    "count": 119,
    "rows": [
      {
        "id": 302,
        "course": "C01",
        "seq": 302,
        "acip": "DE YANG SEMS CAN 'DI DAG LA ZAG MED KYI BDE BA TA GA LA YOD DE, ZAG BCAS KYI BDE BA TZAM YANG MI 'DUG, SEMS CAN KHO RANG TSOS BDE BA YIN SNYAM PA 'DI RNAMS KYANG NGO BO 'GYUR BA'I SDUG BSNGAL SHA STAG YIN, 'DOD RGYU BDE BA 'DOD KYANG BDE BA'I RGYUR DGE BA SGRUB MI SHES, MI 'DOD RGYU SDUG BSNGAL MI 'DOD KYANG , SDUG BSNGAL GYI RGYU SDIG PA SPONG MI SHES, BLANG DOR GO LOG TU SPYAD DE SDUG BSNGAL GYIS NYAM THAG PA'I MA RGAN SEMS CAN",
        "wylie": "de yang sems can 'di dag la zag med kyi bde ba ta ga la yod de, zag bcas kyi bde ba tsam yang mi 'dug, sems can kho rang tshos bde ba yin snyam pa 'di rnams kyang ngo bo 'gyur ba'i sdug bsngal sha stag yin, 'dod rgyu bde ba 'dod kyang bde ba'i rgyur dge ba sgrub mi shes, mi 'dod rgyu sdug bsngal mi 'dod kyang , sdug bsngal gyi rgyu sdig pa spong mi shes, blang dor go log tu spyad de sdug bsngal gyis nyam thag pa'i ma rgan sems can",
        "english": "Right now it's absurd to say that these beings have any kind of pure happiness--they don't even have any of the impure kinds. Every single thing they think is happiness is, in its essence, nothing more than pain. They want wantables but don't want to know about doing the good deeds that bring happiness. They want no unwantables but don't want to know about giving up the bad deeds that bring pain. They act ass backwards: they do what they shouldn't and don't what they should. And so my dear aged mothers, these living beings, are made to suffer.",
        "raw": "{\"acip\": \"DE YANG SEMS CAN 'DI DAG LA ZAG MED KYI BDE BA TA GA LA YOD DE, ZAG BCAS KYI BDE BA TZAM YANG MI 'DUG, SEMS CAN KHO RANG TSOS BDE BA YIN SNYAM PA 'DI RNAMS KYANG NGO BO 'GYUR BA'I SDUG BSNGAL SHA STAG YIN, 'DOD RGYU BDE BA 'DOD KYANG BDE BA'I RGYUR DGE BA SGRUB MI SHES, MI 'DOD RGYU SDUG BSNGAL MI 'DOD KYANG , SDUG BSNGAL GYI RGYU SDIG PA SPONG MI SHES, BLANG DOR GO LOG TU SPYAD DE SDUG BSNGAL GYIS NYAM THAG PA'I MA RGAN SEMS CAN\", \"wylie\": \"de yang sems can 'di dag la zag med kyi bde ba ta ga la yod de, zag bcas kyi bde ba tsam yang mi 'dug, sems can kho rang tshos bde ba yin snyam pa 'di rnams kyang ngo bo 'gyur ba'i sdug bsngal sha stag yin, 'dod rgyu bde ba 'dod kyang bde ba'i rgyur dge ba sgrub mi shes, mi 'dod rgyu sdug bsngal mi 'dod kyang , sdug bsngal gyi rgyu sdig pa spong mi shes, blang dor go log tu spyad de sdug bsngal gyis nyam thag pa'i ma rgan sems can\", \"english\": \"Right now it's absurd to say that these beings have any kind of pure happiness--they don't even have any of the impure kinds. Every single thing they think is happiness is, in its essence, nothing more than pain. They want wantables but don't want to know about doing the good deeds that bring happiness. They want no unwantables but don't want to know about giving up the bad deeds that bring pain. They act ass backwards: they do what they shouldn't and don't what they should. And so my dear aged mothers, these living beings, are made to suffer.\", \"course\": \"C01\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 1420,
        "course": "C05",
        "seq": 38,
        "acip": "SLOB DPON GANG SPEL NI, ZAG BCAS ZAG PA MED CHOS RNAMS, , ZHES PA MDOR BSTAN DU BYAS NAS DE NYID RGYAS PAR 'CHAD PA LA GNAS BRGYAD DU MDZAD DE, DE LA ZAG BCAS ZAG MED SPYIR BSTAN PA'I PHYIR GNAS DANG PO GNYIS BSTAN, ZAG BCAS BYE BRAG TU STON PA'I DBANG DU BYAS NAS GNAS GSUM STE, GANG KUN NAS NYON MONGS PA, GANG DU KUN NAS NYON MONGS PA, JI LTAR KUN NAS NYON MONGS PA GSUM RIM PA LTAR, SEMS CAN GYI 'JIG RTEN KHAMS GSUM 'GRO BA LNGA DANG , SNOD KYI 'JIG RTEN DANG , SKYE GNAS BZHI RTEN 'BREL YAN LAG BCU GNYIS STON PA GNAS GSUM PA DANG , GANG GIS KUN NAS NYON MONGS PAR BYED PA'I ZAG BCAS KYI LAS DANG NYON MONGS PA'I DBANG DU MDZAD NAS, GNAS BZHI PA DANG LNGA PA DANG ,",
        "wylie": "slob dpon gang spel ni, zag bcas zag pa med chos rnams, , zhes pa mdor bstan du byas nas de nyid rgyas par 'chad pa la gnas brgyad du mdzad de, de la zag bcas zag med spyir bstan pa'i phyir gnas dang po gnyis bstan, zag bcas bye brag tu ston pa'i dbang du byas nas gnas gsum ste, gang kun nas nyon mongs pa, gang du kun nas nyon mongs pa, ji ltar kun nas nyon mongs pa gsum rim pa ltar, sems can gyi 'jig rten khams gsum 'gro ba lnga dang , snod kyi 'jig rten dang , skye gnas bzhi rten 'brel yan lag bcu gnyis ston pa gnas gsum pa dang , gang gis kun nas nyon mongs par byed pa'i zag bcas kyi las dang nyon mongs pa'i dbang du mdzad nas, gnas bzhi pa dang lnga pa dang",
        "english": "Master Purnavardhana presents the eight chapters of the Treasure House as follows: Eight chapters were written to elucidate in detail what is indicated only briefly by the opening words of the texts: \"All phenomena, stained or without stain.\" the first two of the chapters are devoted to a general treatment of stained and unstained phenomena. A detailed presentation of stained phenomena is left to the next three chapters. The third, for example, relates (1) who it is that is so very afflicted, (2) where they live so very afflicted, and (3) how they are so very afflicted. It does so with respective presentations on (1) the five types of beings in the three realms--the world of living beings; (2) the external world--the \"vessel\" which holds these beings, and (3) the four modes of birth and twelve links of dependent origination. The fourth and fifth chapters describe what it is that makes beings so very afflicted--stained deeds and the mental afflictions.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"SLOB DPON GANG SPEL NI, ZAG BCAS ZAG PA MED CHOS RNAMS, , ZHES PA MDOR BSTAN DU BYAS NAS DE NYID RGYAS PAR 'CHAD PA LA GNAS BRGYAD DU MDZAD DE, DE LA ZAG BCAS ZAG MED SPYIR BSTAN PA'I PHYIR GNAS DANG PO GNYIS BSTAN, ZAG BCAS BYE BRAG TU STON PA'I DBANG DU BYAS NAS GNAS GSUM STE, GANG KUN NAS NYON MONGS PA, GANG DU KUN NAS NYON MONGS PA, JI LTAR KUN NAS NYON MONGS PA GSUM RIM PA LTAR, SEMS CAN GYI 'JIG RTEN KHAMS GSUM 'GRO BA LNGA DANG , SNOD KYI 'JIG RTEN DANG , SKYE GNAS BZHI RTEN 'BREL YAN LAG BCU GNYIS STON PA GNAS GSUM PA DANG , GANG GIS KUN NAS NYON MONGS PAR BYED PA'I ZAG BCAS KYI LAS DANG NYON MONGS PA'I DBANG DU MDZAD NAS, GNAS BZHI PA DANG LNGA PA DANG ,\", \"wylie\": \"slob dpon gang spel ni, zag bcas zag pa med chos rnams, , zhes pa mdor bstan du byas nas de nyid rgyas par 'chad pa la gnas brgyad du mdzad de, de la zag bcas zag med spyir bstan pa'i phyir gnas dang po gnyis bstan, zag bcas bye brag tu ston pa'i dbang du byas nas gnas gsum ste, gang kun nas nyon mongs pa, gang du kun nas nyon mongs pa, ji ltar kun nas nyon mongs pa gsum rim pa ltar, sems can gyi 'jig rten khams gsum 'gro ba lnga dang , snod kyi 'jig rten dang , skye gnas bzhi rten 'brel yan lag bcu gnyis ston pa gnas gsum pa dang , gang gis kun nas nyon mongs par byed pa'i zag bcas kyi las dang nyon mongs pa'i dbang du mdzad nas, gnas bzhi pa dang lnga pa dang\", \"english\": \"Master Purnavardhana presents the eight chapters of the Treasure House as follows: Eight chapters were written to elucidate in detail what is indicated only briefly by the opening words of the texts: \\\"All phenomena, stained or without stain.\\\" the first two of the chapters are devoted to a general treatment of stained and unstained phenomena. A detailed presentation of stained phenomena is left to the next three chapters. The third, for example, relates (1) who it is that is so very afflicted, (2) where they live so very afflicted, and (3) how they are so very afflicted. It does so with respective presentations on (1) the five types of beings in the three realms--the world of living beings; (2) the external world--the \\\"vessel\\\" which holds these beings, and (3) the four modes of birth and twelve links of dependent origination. The fourth and fifth chapters describe what it is that makes beings so very afflicted--stained deeds and the mental afflictions.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 1421,
        "course": "C05",
        "seq": 39,
        "acip": "ZAG MED BYE BRAG TU STON PA'I DBANG DU MDZAD NAS GNAS PHYI MA GSUM STE, GANG RNAM PAR BYANG BA'I GANG ZAG DANG GANG DU RNAM PAR BYANG BA'I GNAS DANG JI LTAR RNAM PAR BYANG BA'I MNGON PAR RTOGS PA'I RIM PA STON PA BNAS DRUG PA DANG , MDO LAS, NYON MONGS PA DAG NI YE SHES KYIS GZHOM PAR BYA BA YIN NO, , ZHES 'BYUNG BAS GANG GIS RNAM PAR BYANG BAR BYED PA GNAS BDUN PA DANG , MDO LAS, SEMS MNYAM PAR BZHAG PAS NI YANG DAG PA JI LTA BA BZHIN DU RAB TU SHES SO, , ZHES 'BYUNG BAS YE SHES RTEN BRTEN PA'I DBANG DU MDZAD NAS GNAS BRGYAD PA SNYOMS 'JUG BSTAN PA BSHAD DO ZHES 'CHAD DO,",
        "wylie": "zag med bye brag tu ston pa'i dbang du mdzad nas gnas phyi ma gsum ste, gang rnam par byang ba'i gang zag dang gang du rnam par byang ba'i gnas dang ji ltar rnam par byang ba'i mngon par rtogs pa'i rim pa ston pa bnas drug pa dang , mdo las, nyon mongs pa dag ni ye shes kyis gzhom par bya ba yin no, , zhes 'byung bas gang gis rnam par byang bar byed pa gnas bdun pa dang , mdo las, sems mnyam par bzhag pas ni yang dag pa ji lta ba bzhin du rab tu shes so, , zhes 'byung bas ye shes rten brten pa'i dbang du mdzad nas gnas brgyad pa snyoms 'jug bstan pa bshad do zhes 'chad do",
        "english": "The final three chapters give a detailed treatment of unstained phenomena. Chapter Six covers who it is, what kind of person, that is purified; where it is, the place, that he is purified; and how it is, by what stages of realization, that he is purified. Chapter Seven concerns what it is that makes the person pure: as sutra says, \"Affliction is something wisdom must destroy.\" Chapter Eight concerns meditation--that which provides a base for wisdom to rely on--for as sutra states again, \"The mind in meditation gleans pure reality.\"",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"ZAG MED BYE BRAG TU STON PA'I DBANG DU MDZAD NAS GNAS PHYI MA GSUM STE, GANG RNAM PAR BYANG BA'I GANG ZAG DANG GANG DU RNAM PAR BYANG BA'I GNAS DANG JI LTAR RNAM PAR BYANG BA'I MNGON PAR RTOGS PA'I RIM PA STON PA BNAS DRUG PA DANG , MDO LAS, NYON MONGS PA DAG NI YE SHES KYIS GZHOM PAR BYA BA YIN NO, , ZHES 'BYUNG BAS GANG GIS RNAM PAR BYANG BAR BYED PA GNAS BDUN PA DANG , MDO LAS, SEMS MNYAM PAR BZHAG PAS NI YANG DAG PA JI LTA BA BZHIN DU RAB TU SHES SO, , ZHES 'BYUNG BAS YE SHES RTEN BRTEN PA'I DBANG DU MDZAD NAS GNAS BRGYAD PA SNYOMS 'JUG BSTAN PA BSHAD DO ZHES 'CHAD DO,\", \"wylie\": \"zag med bye brag tu ston pa'i dbang du mdzad nas gnas phyi ma gsum ste, gang rnam par byang ba'i gang zag dang gang du rnam par byang ba'i gnas dang ji ltar rnam par byang ba'i mngon par rtogs pa'i rim pa ston pa bnas drug pa dang , mdo las, nyon mongs pa dag ni ye shes kyis gzhom par bya ba yin no, , zhes 'byung bas gang gis rnam par byang bar byed pa gnas bdun pa dang , mdo las, sems mnyam par bzhag pas ni yang dag pa ji lta ba bzhin du rab tu shes so, , zhes 'byung bas ye shes rten brten pa'i dbang du mdzad nas gnas brgyad pa snyoms 'jug bstan pa bshad do zhes 'chad do\", \"english\": \"The final three chapters give a detailed treatment of unstained phenomena. Chapter Six covers who it is, what kind of person, that is purified; where it is, the place, that he is purified; and how it is, by what stages of realization, that he is purified. Chapter Seven concerns what it is that makes the person pure: as sutra says, \\\"Affliction is something wisdom must destroy.\\\" Chapter Eight concerns meditation--that which provides a base for wisdom to rely on--for as sutra states again, \\\"The mind in meditation gleans pure reality.\\\"\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 1423,
        "course": "C05",
        "seq": 41,
        "acip": ",GNYIS PA NI, CHOS MNGON PA'I BRJOD BYA ZAG BCAS ZAG MED STON PA'I DBANG DU MDZAD NAS GNAS DANG PO BSHAD DO, , DER MING TZAM SMOS PA'I DBANG PO DANG 'DUS BYAS KYI SKYE TSUL RGYAS PAR BSTAN PA'I PHYIR DE'I RJES LA GNYIS PA DANG , DE DAG TU KHAMS GSUM GYI MING TZAM SMOS PA RGYAS PAR BSHAD PA'I PHYIR DU DE'I RJES LA GNAS GSUM PA DANG , KHAMS GSUM BSTAN PA DE'I RGYU DBANG PHYUG LA SOGS PA YIN PA BKAG NAS DNGOS SU LAS LAS BYUNG NGO ZHES BSTAN PA'I PHYIR DE'I RJES LA GNAS BZHI PA DANG ,",
        "wylie": "gnyis pa ni, chos mngon pa'i brjod bya zag bcas zag med ston pa'i dbang du mdzad nas gnas dang po bshad do, , der ming tsam smos pa'i dbang po dang 'dus byas kyi skye tshul rgyas par bstan pa'i phyir de'i rjes la gnyis pa dang , de dag tu khams gsum gyi ming tsam smos pa rgyas par bshad pa'i phyir du de'i rjes la gnas gsum pa dang , khams gsum bstan pa de'i rgyu dbang phyug la sogs pa yin pa bkag nas dngos su las las byung ngo zhes bstan pa'i phyir de'i rjes la gnas bzhi pa dang",
        "english": "As for the interrelation of these chapters, the first presents the general subject matter addressed by the works on knowledge: stained and unstained phenomena. The first chapter makes but a mere mention of the powers and of the way in which caused phenomena arise; thus the following chapter, the second, is devoted to a more detailed treatment of these two points. The subject of the three realms, given only passing mention in these opening chapters, is therefore explored in the third. Some believe that the three realms thus presented are creations of some god, such as the one they call \"Powerful\"; the following, fourth chapter therefore disproves this idea and shows that the real source is deeds.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \",GNYIS PA NI, CHOS MNGON PA'I BRJOD BYA ZAG BCAS ZAG MED STON PA'I DBANG DU MDZAD NAS GNAS DANG PO BSHAD DO, , DER MING TZAM SMOS PA'I DBANG PO DANG 'DUS BYAS KYI SKYE TSUL RGYAS PAR BSTAN PA'I PHYIR DE'I RJES LA GNYIS PA DANG , DE DAG TU KHAMS GSUM GYI MING TZAM SMOS PA RGYAS PAR BSHAD PA'I PHYIR DU DE'I RJES LA GNAS GSUM PA DANG , KHAMS GSUM BSTAN PA DE'I RGYU DBANG PHYUG LA SOGS PA YIN PA BKAG NAS DNGOS SU LAS LAS BYUNG NGO ZHES BSTAN PA'I PHYIR DE'I RJES LA GNAS BZHI PA DANG ,\", \"wylie\": \"gnyis pa ni, chos mngon pa'i brjod bya zag bcas zag med ston pa'i dbang du mdzad nas gnas dang po bshad do, , der ming tsam smos pa'i dbang po dang 'dus byas kyi skye tshul rgyas par bstan pa'i phyir de'i rjes la gnyis pa dang , de dag tu khams gsum gyi ming tsam smos pa rgyas par bshad pa'i phyir du de'i rjes la gnas gsum pa dang , khams gsum bstan pa de'i rgyu dbang phyug la sogs pa yin pa bkag nas dngos su las las byung ngo zhes bstan pa'i phyir de'i rjes la gnas bzhi pa dang\", \"english\": \"As for the interrelation of these chapters, the first presents the general subject matter addressed by the works on knowledge: stained and unstained phenomena. The first chapter makes but a mere mention of the powers and of the way in which caused phenomena arise; thus the following chapter, the second, is devoted to a more detailed treatment of these two points. The subject of the three realms, given only passing mention in these opening chapters, is therefore explored in the third. Some believe that the three realms thus presented are creations of some god, such as the one they call \\\"Powerful\\\"; the following, fourth chapter therefore disproves this idea and shows that the real source is deeds.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 1429,
        "course": "C05",
        "seq": 47,
        "acip": "ZAG MED KYI MTHONG SGOM MI SLOB LAM GSUM PO CHOS CAN, DON DAM PA'I CHOS MNGON PA YIN TE, DRI MA MED PA'I SHES RAB RJES 'BRANG DANG BCAS PA YIN PA'I PHYIR,",
        "wylie": "zag med kyi mthong sgom mi slob lam gsum po chos can, don dam pa'i chos mngon pa yin te, dri ma med pa'i shes rab rjes 'brang dang bcas pa yin pa'i phyir",
        "english": "Consider the following three paths without stain: those of seeing, habituation, and no further learning. They are actual knowledge, because They constitute unstained wisdom and its accessories.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"ZAG MED KYI MTHONG SGOM MI SLOB LAM GSUM PO CHOS CAN, DON DAM PA'I CHOS MNGON PA YIN TE, DRI MA MED PA'I SHES RAB RJES 'BRANG DANG BCAS PA YIN PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"zag med kyi mthong sgom mi slob lam gsum po chos can, don dam pa'i chos mngon pa yin te, dri ma med pa'i shes rab rjes 'brang dang bcas pa yin pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Consider the following three paths without stain: those of seeing, habituation, and no further learning. They are actual knowledge, because They constitute unstained wisdom and its accessories.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 1432,
        "course": "C05",
        "seq": 50,
        "acip": "DE NYER BSDOGS LHAG MA RNAMS LA MI BRTEN TE, DE ZAG MED YIN LA NYER BSDOGS LHAG MA RNAMS ZAG BCAS ZHI RAGS KYI RNAM PA CAN YIN PA'I PHYIR, 'DOD PA LA MI BRTEN TE, 'DI MNYAM PAR BZHAG PA'I SA PA YIN LA, 'DOD PA MNYAM PAR MA BZHAG PA'I SA PA YIN PA'I PHYIR,",
        "wylie": "de nyer bsdogs lhag ma rnams la mi brten te, de zag med yin la nyer bsdogs lhag ma rnams zag bcas zhi rags kyi rnam pa can yin pa'i phyir, 'dod pa la mi brten te, 'di mnyam par bzhag pa'i sa pa yin la, 'dod pa mnyam par ma bzhag pa'i sa pa yin pa'i phyir",
        "english": "The ultimate-phenomenon stage of the path of preparation does not, however, rely on preliminary levels other than the one mentioned. The stage beyond no leisure is free of stain, whereas the remaining stages are stained--they are levels wherein one's state of realization derives only from making distinctions between more or less subtle levels of experience. Nor can the ultimate stage of the path of preparation ever rely on the desire level, for the stage is one of controlled meditation, and the desire level is not.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"DE NYER BSDOGS LHAG MA RNAMS LA MI BRTEN TE, DE ZAG MED YIN LA NYER BSDOGS LHAG MA RNAMS ZAG BCAS ZHI RAGS KYI RNAM PA CAN YIN PA'I PHYIR, 'DOD PA LA MI BRTEN TE, 'DI MNYAM PAR BZHAG PA'I SA PA YIN LA, 'DOD PA MNYAM PAR MA BZHAG PA'I SA PA YIN PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"de nyer bsdogs lhag ma rnams la mi brten te, de zag med yin la nyer bsdogs lhag ma rnams zag bcas zhi rags kyi rnam pa can yin pa'i phyir, 'dod pa la mi brten te, 'di mnyam par bzhag pa'i sa pa yin la, 'dod pa mnyam par ma bzhag pa'i sa pa yin pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"The ultimate-phenomenon stage of the path of preparation does not, however, rely on preliminary levels other than the one mentioned. The stage beyond no leisure is free of stain, whereas the remaining stages are stained--they are levels wherein one's state of realization derives only from making distinctions between more or less subtle levels of experience. Nor can the ultimate stage of the path of preparation ever rely on the desire level, for the stage is one of controlled meditation, and the desire level is not.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 1434,
        "course": "C05",
        "seq": 52,
        "acip": "ZAG MED SGOM LAM DANG MI SLOB LAM NI ZAG MED SA DGU GANG RUNG LA BRTEN NO,",
        "wylie": "zag med sgom lam dang mi slob lam ni zag med sa dgu gang rung la brten no",
        "english": "The unstained path of habituation, as well as that of no further learning, may both rely on any one of the nine unstained levels.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"ZAG MED SGOM LAM DANG MI SLOB LAM NI ZAG MED SA DGU GANG RUNG LA BRTEN NO,\", \"wylie\": \"zag med sgom lam dang mi slob lam ni zag med sa dgu gang rung la brten no\", \"english\": \"The unstained path of habituation, as well as that of no further learning, may both rely on any one of the nine unstained levels.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 1436,
        "course": "C05",
        "seq": 54,
        "acip": ",GSUM PA NI, 'KHOR DANG BCAS NA PHUNG PO DU BA YIN ZHE NA, ZAG MED KYI SHES RAB KYI 'KHOR DU ZAG MED KYI SDOM PAS BSDUS PA'I RNAM PAR RIG BYED MA YIN PA'I GZUGS KYANG YOD PAS PHUNG PO LNGA PA CAN YIN LA, DE YANG BSAM GTAN DANG PO GNYIS LA BRTEN NAS YID BDE DANG GSUM PA LA BRTEN NAS SEMS BDE, BTANG SNYOMS KYI SA GZHAN GANG RUNG LA BRTEN NAS TSOR BA BTANG SNYOMS DANG , YID SHES 'KHOR DU BYUNG BA'I 'DU SHES, SEMS BYUNG 'DU BYED KYI PHUNG PO, LDAN MIN 'DU BYED CI RIGS PA YID KYI RNAM SHES DANG BCAS PA RNAMS YOD PA'I PHYIR,",
        "wylie": "gsum pa ni, 'khor dang bcas na phung po du ba yin zhe na, zag med kyi shes rab kyi 'khor du zag med kyi sdom pas bsdus pa'i rnam par rig byed ma yin pa'i gzugs kyang yod pas phung po lnga pa can yin la, de yang bsam gtan dang po gnyis la brten nas yid bde dang gsum pa la brten nas sems bde, btang snyoms kyi sa gzhan gang rung la brten nas tshor ba btang snyoms dang , yid shes 'khor du byung ba'i 'du shes, sems byung 'du byed kyi phung po, ldan min 'du byed ci rigs pa yid kyi rnam shes dang bcas pa rnams yod pa'i phyir",
        "english": "How many heaps does knowledge have, if you count all of those which are attendant to it? Knowledge can be said to have all five heaps, since unstained wisdom comes along with non-communicating form included within unstained restraint. Feeling is present as pleasure of thought if the wisdom relies on the first two concentration levels. Where it relies on the third, mental pleasure is had. Reliance on other levels, of neutral feeling, means that neutral feeling is present. Discrimination is there as an attendant to consciousness of one's thoughts, as well as the heap of other factors--be they mental functions or the factors not associated with mind--along with consciousness.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \",GSUM PA NI, 'KHOR DANG BCAS NA PHUNG PO DU BA YIN ZHE NA, ZAG MED KYI SHES RAB KYI 'KHOR DU ZAG MED KYI SDOM PAS BSDUS PA'I RNAM PAR RIG BYED MA YIN PA'I GZUGS KYANG YOD PAS PHUNG PO LNGA PA CAN YIN LA, DE YANG BSAM GTAN DANG PO GNYIS LA BRTEN NAS YID BDE DANG GSUM PA LA BRTEN NAS SEMS BDE, BTANG SNYOMS KYI SA GZHAN GANG RUNG LA BRTEN NAS TSOR BA BTANG SNYOMS DANG , YID SHES 'KHOR DU BYUNG BA'I 'DU SHES, SEMS BYUNG 'DU BYED KYI PHUNG PO, LDAN MIN 'DU BYED CI RIGS PA YID KYI RNAM SHES DANG BCAS PA RNAMS YOD PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"gsum pa ni, 'khor dang bcas na phung po du ba yin zhe na, zag med kyi shes rab kyi 'khor du zag med kyi sdom pas bsdus pa'i rnam par rig byed ma yin pa'i gzugs kyang yod pas phung po lnga pa can yin la, de yang bsam gtan dang po gnyis la brten nas yid bde dang gsum pa la brten nas sems bde, btang snyoms kyi sa gzhan gang rung la brten nas tshor ba btang snyoms dang , yid shes 'khor du byung ba'i 'du shes, sems byung 'du byed kyi phung po, ldan min 'du byed ci rigs pa yid kyi rnam shes dang bcas pa rnams yod pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"How many heaps does knowledge have, if you count all of those which are attendant to it? Knowledge can be said to have all five heaps, since unstained wisdom comes along with non-communicating form included within unstained restraint. Feeling is present as pleasure of thought if the wisdom relies on the first two concentration levels. Where it relies on the third, mental pleasure is had. Reliance on other levels, of neutral feeling, means that neutral feeling is present. Discrimination is there as an attendant to consciousness of one's thoughts, as well as the heap of other factors--be they mental functions or the factors not associated with mind--along with consciousness.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 1437,
        "course": "C05",
        "seq": 55,
        "acip": "KHO NA RE, YID KYI RNAM SHES ZAG MED KYI SHES RAB KYI 'KHOR DU SMOS PA MI 'THAD DE, SEMS BYUNG LAS SEMS GTZO CHE BA'I PHYIR ZHE NA, SKYON MED DE, SPYIR DE LTAR YIN YANG CHOS RAB TU RNAM 'BYED KYI SKABS SU SHES RAB GTZO CHE BA YIN PA'I PHYIR, DPER NA, YID CHES PA'I GNAS SKABS NA DAD PA GTZO CHE BA BZHIN NO,",
        "wylie": "kho na re, yid kyi rnam shes zag med kyi shes rab kyi 'khor du smos pa mi 'thad de, sems byung las sems gtso che ba'i phyir zhe na, skyon med de, spyir de ltar yin yang chos rab tu rnam 'byed kyi skabs su shes rab gtso che ba yin pa'i phyir, dper na, yid ches pa'i gnas skabs na dad pa gtso che ba bzhin no",
        "english": "Someone may object that it is improper to speak of consciousness of one's thoughts as being \"attendant\" to unstained wisdom, since mind itself must be considered more primary than mental functions. Generally the relationship is so, but in the context of an ultimate analysis of phenomena wisdom must be treated as the more primary. If the context had been the subject of belief, for example, we could even have considered faith more principal.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"KHO NA RE, YID KYI RNAM SHES ZAG MED KYI SHES RAB KYI 'KHOR DU SMOS PA MI 'THAD DE, SEMS BYUNG LAS SEMS GTZO CHE BA'I PHYIR ZHE NA, SKYON MED DE, SPYIR DE LTAR YIN YANG CHOS RAB TU RNAM 'BYED KYI SKABS SU SHES RAB GTZO CHE BA YIN PA'I PHYIR, DPER NA, YID CHES PA'I GNAS SKABS NA DAD PA GTZO CHE BA BZHIN NO,\", \"wylie\": \"kho na re, yid kyi rnam shes zag med kyi shes rab kyi 'khor du smos pa mi 'thad de, sems byung las sems gtso che ba'i phyir zhe na, skyon med de, spyir de ltar yin yang chos rab tu rnam 'byed kyi skabs su shes rab gtso che ba yin pa'i phyir, dper na, yid ches pa'i gnas skabs na dad pa gtso che ba bzhin no\", \"english\": \"Someone may object that it is improper to speak of consciousness of one's thoughts as being \\\"attendant\\\" to unstained wisdom, since mind itself must be considered more primary than mental functions. Generally the relationship is so, but in the context of an ultimate analysis of phenomena wisdom must be treated as the more primary. If the context had been the subject of belief, for example, we could even have considered faith more principal.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 1441,
        "course": "C05",
        "seq": 59,
        "acip": ",DE RNAMS CHOS CAN, KHYOD LA CHOS MNGON PA'I MING GIS 'DOGS PA'I RGYU MTSAN YOD DE, KHYOD DON DAM PA'I CHOS MNGON PA THOB PA'I THABS SAM RGYU YIN PA'I PHYIR, DE YANG RGYUR 'GYUR TSUL NI SKYES THOB KYI SHES RAB LA BRTEN NAS, THOS BYUNG , DE LAS BSAM BYUNG , DE LAS SGOM BYUNG , DE LAS ZAG MED KYI SHES RAB SKYE BA'I PHYIR,",
        "wylie": "de rnams chos can, khyod la chos mngon pa'i ming gis 'dogs pa'i rgyu mtshan yod de, khyod don dam pa'i chos mngon pa thob pa'i thabs sam rgyu yin pa'i phyir, de yang rgyur 'gyur tshul ni skyes thob kyi shes rab la brten nas, thos byung , de las bsam byung , de las sgom byung , de las zag med kyi shes rab skye ba'i phyir",
        "english": "Let us use the form of a logical statement: Consider the accessories to knowledge just mentioned. They may be given the name \"knowledge,\" because They constitute the means or cause of one's achieving actual knowledge.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \",DE RNAMS CHOS CAN, KHYOD LA CHOS MNGON PA'I MING GIS 'DOGS PA'I RGYU MTSAN YOD DE, KHYOD DON DAM PA'I CHOS MNGON PA THOB PA'I THABS SAM RGYU YIN PA'I PHYIR, DE YANG RGYUR 'GYUR TSUL NI SKYES THOB KYI SHES RAB LA BRTEN NAS, THOS BYUNG , DE LAS BSAM BYUNG , DE LAS SGOM BYUNG , DE LAS ZAG MED KYI SHES RAB SKYE BA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"de rnams chos can, khyod la chos mngon pa'i ming gis 'dogs pa'i rgyu mtshan yod de, khyod don dam pa'i chos mngon pa thob pa'i thabs sam rgyu yin pa'i phyir, de yang rgyur 'gyur tshul ni skyes thob kyi shes rab la brten nas, thos byung , de las bsam byung , de las sgom byung , de las zag med kyi shes rab skye ba'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Let us use the form of a logical statement: Consider the accessories to knowledge just mentioned. They may be given the name \\\"knowledge,\\\" because They constitute the means or cause of one's achieving actual knowledge.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 1465,
        "course": "C05",
        "seq": 83,
        "acip": "ZAG BCAS ZAG MED KYI CHOS RNAMS BSTAN BCOS KYI BRJOD BYA YIN ZHING DE NI CHOS RNAMS ZHES PAS BSTAN, DE LA BRTEN NAS SLOB MA'I RGYUD LA CHOS RAB TU RNAM 'BYED KYI SHES RAB BSKYED PA NI DGOS PA YIN ZHING DE NI RAB TU RNAM 'BYED CES PAS BSTAN, DE LA BRTEN NAS LHAG BCAS LHAG MED KYI MYANG 'DAS THOB PA NYID DGOS YIN ZHING DE NI, MED PAR NYON MONGS RNAMS ZHES PA NAS, DE BAS ZHES PA'I BAR GYIS SHUGS LA BSTAN NYING DGOS DGOS PA, DGOS PA BSTAN BCOS KYI BRJOD BYA LA RAG LAS PA NI 'BREL PA'O, , YANG NA CHOS RNAMS BRJOD BYA DANG , BSTAN BCOS 'DI RJOD BYED DU 'BREL PA NI 'BREL PA'O,",
        "wylie": "zag bcas zag med kyi chos rnams bstan bcos kyi brjod bya yin zhing de ni chos rnams zhes pas bstan, de la brten nas slob ma'i rgyud la chos rab tu rnam 'byed kyi shes rab bskyed pa ni dgos pa yin zhing de ni rab tu rnam 'byed ces pas bstan, de la brten nas lhag bcas lhag med kyi myang 'das thob pa nyid dgos yin zhing de ni, med par nyon mongs rnams zhes pa nas, de bas zhes pa'i bar gyis shugs la bstan nying dgos dgos pa, dgos pa bstan bcos kyi brjod bya la rag las pa ni 'brel pa'o, , yang na chos rnams brjod bya dang , bstan bcos 'di rjod byed du 'brel pa ni 'brel pa'o",
        "english": "Here are the four attributes: 1) The subject matter of the text concerns stained and unstained types of phenomena. It is indicated in the verse with the words \"every existing phenomenon.\" 2) The purpose is to utilize this subject matter to plant, in students' minds, that wisdom which analyzes phenomena in an ultimate way. It is indicated by the words \"ultimate analysis.\" 3) The ultimate goal is to have these students achieve nirvana, both with and without anything remaining. It is indicated indirectly by the entire phrase running from \"There's no way...\" up to \"...without,\" and on from \"this affliction...\" up to the word \"Thus.\" 4) A relation exists in that the purpose must be achieved through the subject matter of the work, and the ultimate goal through this very purpose. One may also state the relation as being between the subject matter of the work (that is, all existing phenomena) and the means by which this subject matter is expressed (i.e., the commentary itself).",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"ZAG BCAS ZAG MED KYI CHOS RNAMS BSTAN BCOS KYI BRJOD BYA YIN ZHING DE NI CHOS RNAMS ZHES PAS BSTAN, DE LA BRTEN NAS SLOB MA'I RGYUD LA CHOS RAB TU RNAM 'BYED KYI SHES RAB BSKYED PA NI DGOS PA YIN ZHING DE NI RAB TU RNAM 'BYED CES PAS BSTAN, DE LA BRTEN NAS LHAG BCAS LHAG MED KYI MYANG 'DAS THOB PA NYID DGOS YIN ZHING DE NI, MED PAR NYON MONGS RNAMS ZHES PA NAS, DE BAS ZHES PA'I BAR GYIS SHUGS LA BSTAN NYING DGOS DGOS PA, DGOS PA BSTAN BCOS KYI BRJOD BYA LA RAG LAS PA NI 'BREL PA'O, , YANG NA CHOS RNAMS BRJOD BYA DANG , BSTAN BCOS 'DI RJOD BYED DU 'BREL PA NI 'BREL PA'O,\", \"wylie\": \"zag bcas zag med kyi chos rnams bstan bcos kyi brjod bya yin zhing de ni chos rnams zhes pas bstan, de la brten nas slob ma'i rgyud la chos rab tu rnam 'byed kyi shes rab bskyed pa ni dgos pa yin zhing de ni rab tu rnam 'byed ces pas bstan, de la brten nas lhag bcas lhag med kyi myang 'das thob pa nyid dgos yin zhing de ni, med par nyon mongs rnams zhes pa nas, de bas zhes pa'i bar gyis shugs la bstan nying dgos dgos pa, dgos pa bstan bcos kyi brjod bya la rag las pa ni 'brel pa'o, , yang na chos rnams brjod bya dang , bstan bcos 'di rjod byed du 'brel pa ni 'brel pa'o\", \"english\": \"Here are the four attributes: 1) The subject matter of the text concerns stained and unstained types of phenomena. It is indicated in the verse with the words \\\"every existing phenomenon.\\\" 2) The purpose is to utilize this subject matter to plant, in students' minds, that wisdom which analyzes phenomena in an ultimate way. It is indicated by the words \\\"ultimate analysis.\\\" 3) The ultimate goal is to have these students achieve nirvana, both with and without anything remaining. It is indicated indirectly by the entire phrase running from \\\"There's no way...\\\" up to \\\"...without,\\\" and on from \\\"this affliction...\\\" up to the word \\\"Thus.\\\" 4) A relation exists in that the purpose must be achieved through the subject matter of the work, and the ultimate goal through this very purpose. One may also state the relation as being between the subject matter of the work (that is, all existing phenomena) and the means by which this subject matter is expressed (i.e., the commentary itself).\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 1601,
        "course": "C05",
        "seq": 219,
        "acip": ",'O NA MTHONG LAM BAR CHAD MED LAM GYI DUS SU DE'I SPANG BYA MTHONG SPANG MED MOD KYANG , SGOM SPANG GI BAG LA NYAL YOD DGOS LA, DE'I TSE YID KYI SHES PA NI GNYIS SNANG 'KHRUL PA'I BAG CHAGS KYIS MA BSLAD PA'I ZAG MED YIN PAS, DE'I NGO BOR BAG LA NYAL DE GNAS PA MI RIGS LA, DBANG SHES KYANG DE'I RTEN DU MED CING GZUGS KYANG DE'I RTEN DU MI RIGS LA KUN GZHI YANG MI 'DOD PAS DE'I RTEN MED PAR 'GYUR RO SNYAM NA, SKYON MED DE DE'I TSE NGA TZAM ZHIG SGOM SPANG GI BAG LA NYAL GYI RTEN YIN PA'I PHYIR TE, SPANG GNYEN GZHAN LA YANG DE BZHIN DU SHES PAR BYA'O,",
        "wylie": "'o na mthong lam bar chad med lam gyi dus su de'i spang bya mthong spang med mod kyang , sgom spang gi bag la nyal yod dgos la, de'i tshe yid kyi shes pa ni gnyis snang 'khrul pa'i bag chags kyis ma bslad pa'i zag med yin pas, de'i ngo bor bag la nyal de gnas pa mi rigs la, dbang shes kyang de'i rten du med cing gzugs kyang de'i rten du mi rigs la kun gzhi yang mi 'dod pas de'i rten med par 'gyur ro snyam na, skyon med de de'i tshe nga tsam zhig sgom spang gi bag la nyal gyi rten yin pa'i phyir te, spang gnyen gzhan la yang de bzhin du shes par bya'o",
        "english": "One might wonder about something else: Let's talk about the period while you are in the \"uninterrupted\" stage of the path of seeing [the actual direct perception of emptiness]. I can accept that at this point the negative thoughts eliminated by this path are no longer present, but we would have to say that the negative things eliminated by the path of habituation are still there, in a dormant way. At this particular point in time, the consciousness of the mind is unstained, unaffected by the mental seed which causes the mistaken state of mind where the appearance and actuality of things are different from one another. As such none of these things could lie dormant here, due to its very quality at the time. There is no consciousness of the senses that could act as the basis for those mental seeds, and it would be improper to say that physical form could ever provide such a basis either. According to you, moreover, there's no foundation consciousness. I would have to think then that there is no basis at all where these dormant things could stay. And yet there is no such problem, for at this point in time it is the simple \"me\" which is providing the basis for the dormant things eliminated by the path of habituation to stay. You can apply this reasoning as well to all the other cases involving things to be eliminated, and the antidotes which eliminate them. ********* Selection Three: The Middle-Way School on the question of where the seeds of karma stay until they give their resu",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \",'O NA MTHONG LAM BAR CHAD MED LAM GYI DUS SU DE'I SPANG BYA MTHONG SPANG MED MOD KYANG , SGOM SPANG GI BAG LA NYAL YOD DGOS LA, DE'I TSE YID KYI SHES PA NI GNYIS SNANG 'KHRUL PA'I BAG CHAGS KYIS MA BSLAD PA'I ZAG MED YIN PAS, DE'I NGO BOR BAG LA NYAL DE GNAS PA MI RIGS LA, DBANG SHES KYANG DE'I RTEN DU MED CING GZUGS KYANG DE'I RTEN DU MI RIGS LA KUN GZHI YANG MI 'DOD PAS DE'I RTEN MED PAR 'GYUR RO SNYAM NA, SKYON MED DE DE'I TSE NGA TZAM ZHIG SGOM SPANG GI BAG LA NYAL GYI RTEN YIN PA'I PHYIR TE, SPANG GNYEN GZHAN LA YANG DE BZHIN DU SHES PAR BYA'O,\", \"wylie\": \"'o na mthong lam bar chad med lam gyi dus su de'i spang bya mthong spang med mod kyang , sgom spang gi bag la nyal yod dgos la, de'i tshe yid kyi shes pa ni gnyis snang 'khrul pa'i bag chags kyis ma bslad pa'i zag med yin pas, de'i ngo bor bag la nyal de gnas pa mi rigs la, dbang shes kyang de'i rten du med cing gzugs kyang de'i rten du mi rigs la kun gzhi yang mi 'dod pas de'i rten med par 'gyur ro snyam na, skyon med de de'i tshe nga tsam zhig sgom spang gi bag la nyal gyi rten yin pa'i phyir te, spang gnyen gzhan la yang de bzhin du shes par bya'o\", \"english\": \"One might wonder about something else: Let's talk about the period while you are in the \\\"uninterrupted\\\" stage of the path of seeing [the actual direct perception of emptiness]. I can accept that at this point the negative thoughts eliminated by this path are no longer present, but we would have to say that the negative things eliminated by the path of habituation are still there, in a dormant way. At this particular point in time, the consciousness of the mind is unstained, unaffected by the mental seed which causes the mistaken state of mind where the appearance and actuality of things are different from one another. As such none of these things could lie dormant here, due to its very quality at the time. There is no consciousness of the senses that could act as the basis for those mental seeds, and it would be improper to say that physical form could ever provide such a basis either. According to you, moreover, there's no foundation consciousness. I would have to think then that there is no basis at all where these dormant things could stay. And yet there is no such problem, for at this point in time it is the simple \\\"me\\\" which is providing the basis for the dormant things eliminated by the path of habituation to stay. You can apply this reasoning as well to all the other cases involving things to be eliminated, and the antidotes which eliminate them. ********* Selection Three: The Middle-Way School on the question of where the seeds of karma stay until they give their resu\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 1698,
        "course": "C05",
        "seq": 316,
        "acip": ",MI DGE GZUGS DANG 'DOD GTOGS PA'I, ,DGE BA NYID NI RIM BZHIN DU, ,GNAG DANG DKAR DANG GNYIS KA'I LAS, ,DE ZAD BYED PA ZAG MED YIN,",
        "wylie": "mi dge gzugs dang 'dod gtogs pa'i, ,dge ba nyid ni rim bzhin du, ,gnag dang dkar dang gnyis ka'i las, ,de zad byed pa zag med yin",
        "english": "Non-virtue as well as virtue itself taken In by the form and desire represent Respectively deeds which are black, white, and both. The unstained is what brings it to an end.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \",MI DGE GZUGS DANG 'DOD GTOGS PA'I, ,DGE BA NYID NI RIM BZHIN DU, ,GNAG DANG DKAR DANG GNYIS KA'I LAS, ,DE ZAD BYED PA ZAG MED YIN,\", \"wylie\": \"mi dge gzugs dang 'dod gtogs pa'i, ,dge ba nyid ni rim bzhin du, ,gnag dang dkar dang gnyis ka'i las, ,de zad byed pa zag med yin\", \"english\": \"Non-virtue as well as virtue itself taken In by the form and desire represent Respectively deeds which are black, white, and both. The unstained is what brings it to an end.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 1702,
        "course": "C05",
        "seq": 320,
        "acip": "ZAG MED KYI LAS NI MI DKAR MI GNAG RNAM PAR MI SMIN LA, NAG PO DE ZAD PAR BYED PA YIN NO,",
        "wylie": "zag med kyi las ni mi dkar mi gnag rnam par mi smin la, nag po de zad par byed pa yin no",
        "english": "Deeds which are unstained never ripen into anything either white or black. Their nature is to be what brings \"it\"--that is, the black kind of deed--to an end. 64 Bringing Black and White Deeds to an End",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"ZAG MED KYI LAS NI MI DKAR MI GNAG RNAM PAR MI SMIN LA, NAG PO DE ZAD PAR BYED PA YIN NO,\", \"wylie\": \"zag med kyi las ni mi dkar mi gnag rnam par mi smin la, nag po de zad par byed pa yin no\", \"english\": \"Deeds which are unstained never ripen into anything either white or black. Their nature is to be what brings \\\"it\\\"--that is, the black kind of deed--to an end. 64 Bringing Black and White Deeds to an End\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 1766,
        "course": "C05",
        "seq": 384,
        "acip": ",BAG MED DANG BSKAL BA MI MNYAM PAS GCOD PA MIN ZER YANG , SKAL BA MNYAM MI MNYAM ZAG BCAS ZAG MED LA DMIGS PA KUN GYIS GCOD PAR BYED,",
        "wylie": "bag med dang bskal ba mi mnyam pas gcod pa min zer yang , skal ba mnyam mi mnyam zag bcas zag med la dmigs pa kun gyis gcod par byed",
        "english": "Now some make the claim that views involving unstained phenomena or levels which are not comparable cannot cause a person to lose his most basic virtues. The fact though is that this virtue can be lost through all the forms of this view: through those that focus on levels that are comparable or those which are not; through those that focus on unstained phenomena or on those with stain.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \",BAG MED DANG BSKAL BA MI MNYAM PAS GCOD PA MIN ZER YANG , SKAL BA MNYAM MI MNYAM ZAG BCAS ZAG MED LA DMIGS PA KUN GYIS GCOD PAR BYED,\", \"wylie\": \"bag med dang bskal ba mi mnyam pas gcod pa min zer yang , skal ba mnyam mi mnyam zag bcas zag med la dmigs pa kun gyis gcod par byed\", \"english\": \"Now some make the claim that views involving unstained phenomena or levels which are not comparable cannot cause a person to lose his most basic virtues. The fact though is that this virtue can be lost through all the forms of this view: through those that focus on levels that are comparable or those which are not; through those that focus on unstained phenomena or on those with stain.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 2126,
        "course": "C07",
        "seq": 56,
        "acip": "DE YANG SEMS CAN 'DI DAG LA ZAG MED KYI BDE BA TA GA LA YOD DE, ZAG BCAS KYI BDE BA TZAM YANG MI 'DUG, SEMS CAN KHO RANG TSOS BDE BA YIN SNYAM PA 'DI RNAMS KYANG NGO BO 'GYUR BA'I SDUG BSNGAL SHA STAG YIN, 'DOD RGYU BDE BA 'DOD KYANG BDE BA'I RGYUR DGE BA SGRUB MI SHES, MI 'DOD RGYU SDUG BSNGAL MI 'DOD KYANG , SDUG BSNGAL GYI RGYU SDIG PA SPONG MI SHES, BLANG DOR GO LOG TU SPYAD DE SDUG BSNGAL GYIS NYAM THAG PA'I MA RGAN SEMS CAN 'DI DAG THAMS CAD BDE BA DANG BDE BA'I RGYU THAMS CAD DANG LDAN NA CI MA RUNG , LDAN PAR GYUR CIG, LDAN PA ZHIG NGA RANG GIS BYED, SEMS CAN THAMS CAD SDUG BSNGAL DANG SDUG BSNGAL GYI RGYU THAMS CAD DANG BRAL NA CI MA RUNG , BRAL BAR GYUR CIG, BRAL BA ZHIG NGA RANG GIS BYED SNYAM DU YANG YANG BSGOMS PAS BYAMS PA DANG SNYING RJE SHUGS CAN BSKYED,",
        "wylie": "de yang sems can 'di dag la zag med kyi bde ba ta ga la yod de, zag bcas kyi bde ba tsam yang mi 'dug, sems can kho rang tshos bde ba yin snyam pa 'di rnams kyang ngo bo 'gyur ba'i sdug bsngal sha stag yin, 'dod rgyu bde ba 'dod kyang bde ba'i rgyur dge ba sgrub mi shes, mi 'dod rgyu sdug bsngal mi 'dod kyang , sdug bsngal gyi rgyu sdig pa spong mi shes, blang dor go log tu spyad de sdug bsngal gyis nyam thag pa'i ma rgan sems can 'di dag thams cad bde ba dang bde ba'i rgyu thams cad dang ldan na ci ma rung , ldan par gyur cig, ldan pa zhig nga rang gis byed, sems can thams cad sdug bsngal dang sdug bsngal gyi rgyu thams cad dang bral na ci ma rung , bral bar gyur cig, bral ba zhig nga rang gis byed snyam du yang yang bsgoms pas byams pa dang snying rje shugs can bskyed",
        "english": "Right now it's absurd to say that these beings have any kind of pure happiness--they don't even have any of the impure kinds. Every single thing they think is happiness is, in its essence, nothing more than pain. They want wantables but don't want to know about doing the good deeds that bring happiness. They want no unwantables but don't want to know about giving up the bad deeds that bring pain. They act ass backwards: they do what they shouldn't and don't what they should. And so my dear aged mothers, these living beings, are made to suffer. \"How good it would be if they could all find every happiness, and every cause of happiness. I wish they could. I'll see that they do.\" \"How good it would be if they could all be free of every pain, and every cause of pain. I wish they could. I'll see that they do.\" Let these two trains of thought run through your mind; meditate on them over and over again. Then you will come to feel the very strongest loving-kindness and compassion.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C07\", \"acip\": \"DE YANG SEMS CAN 'DI DAG LA ZAG MED KYI BDE BA TA GA LA YOD DE, ZAG BCAS KYI BDE BA TZAM YANG MI 'DUG, SEMS CAN KHO RANG TSOS BDE BA YIN SNYAM PA 'DI RNAMS KYANG NGO BO 'GYUR BA'I SDUG BSNGAL SHA STAG YIN, 'DOD RGYU BDE BA 'DOD KYANG BDE BA'I RGYUR DGE BA SGRUB MI SHES, MI 'DOD RGYU SDUG BSNGAL MI 'DOD KYANG , SDUG BSNGAL GYI RGYU SDIG PA SPONG MI SHES, BLANG DOR GO LOG TU SPYAD DE SDUG BSNGAL GYIS NYAM THAG PA'I MA RGAN SEMS CAN 'DI DAG THAMS CAD BDE BA DANG BDE BA'I RGYU THAMS CAD DANG LDAN NA CI MA RUNG , LDAN PAR GYUR CIG, LDAN PA ZHIG NGA RANG GIS BYED, SEMS CAN THAMS CAD SDUG BSNGAL DANG SDUG BSNGAL GYI RGYU THAMS CAD DANG BRAL NA CI MA RUNG , BRAL BAR GYUR CIG, BRAL BA ZHIG NGA RANG GIS BYED SNYAM DU YANG YANG BSGOMS PAS BYAMS PA DANG SNYING RJE SHUGS CAN BSKYED,\", \"wylie\": \"de yang sems can 'di dag la zag med kyi bde ba ta ga la yod de, zag bcas kyi bde ba tsam yang mi 'dug, sems can kho rang tshos bde ba yin snyam pa 'di rnams kyang ngo bo 'gyur ba'i sdug bsngal sha stag yin, 'dod rgyu bde ba 'dod kyang bde ba'i rgyur dge ba sgrub mi shes, mi 'dod rgyu sdug bsngal mi 'dod kyang , sdug bsngal gyi rgyu sdig pa spong mi shes, blang dor go log tu spyad de sdug bsngal gyis nyam thag pa'i ma rgan sems can 'di dag thams cad bde ba dang bde ba'i rgyu thams cad dang ldan na ci ma rung , ldan par gyur cig, ldan pa zhig nga rang gis byed, sems can thams cad sdug bsngal dang sdug bsngal gyi rgyu thams cad dang bral na ci ma rung , bral bar gyur cig, bral ba zhig nga rang gis byed snyam du yang yang bsgoms pas byams pa dang snying rje shugs can bskyed\", \"english\": \"Right now it's absurd to say that these beings have any kind of pure happiness--they don't even have any of the impure kinds. Every single thing they think is happiness is, in its essence, nothing more than pain. They want wantables but don't want to know about doing the good deeds that bring happiness. They want no unwantables but don't want to know about giving up the bad deeds that bring pain. They act ass backwards: they do what they shouldn't and don't what they should. And so my dear aged mothers, these living beings, are made to suffer. \\\"How good it would be if they could all find every happiness, and every cause of happiness. I wish they could. I'll see that they do.\\\" \\\"How good it would be if they could all be free of every pain, and every cause of pain. I wish they could. I'll see that they do.\\\" Let these two trains of thought run through your mind; meditate on them over and over again. Then you will come to feel the very strongest loving-kindness and compassion.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 2144,
        "course": "C07",
        "seq": 74,
        "acip": ",CI'I PHYIR TSUL KHRIMS GSUM DU BYAS SHE NA, NYAN THOS KYI THEG PA LA NYON MONGS RTZE 'JIL BA'I SO THAR DANG RKYEN YOD KYANG RE ZHIG MI LDANG BA'I BSAM GTAN GYI SDOM PA DANG , RGYUN GCOD PA'I ZAG MED KYI SDOM PA GSUM YOD PA DANG GRANGS MTHUN PAR BYAS PA'O ZHES 'GREL PA RNAMS LAS 'CHAD MOD KYANG ,",
        "wylie": "ci'i phyir tshul khrims gsum du byas she na, nyan thos kyi theg pa la nyon mongs rtse 'jil ba'i so thar dang rkyen yod kyang re zhig mi ldang ba'i bsam gtan gyi sdom pa dang , rgyun gcod pa'i zag med kyi sdom pa gsum yod pa dang grangs mthun par byas pa'o zhes 'grel pa rnams las 'chad mod kyang",
        "english": "\"Why is it,\" one may ask, \"that you make this division into three different types of morality?\" Admittedly there are commentaries which explain that this division into three is meant to coincide with the division made in the way of the listeners, where the vows are described as three: 1) The vows of individual freedom, which function to take the soften the mental afflictions; 2) The vows of meditation, where given the right circumstances the mental afflictions could still arise but don't for the time being, since one is in a state of meditation; and 3) The unstained vows, where the mental afflictions are discontinued completely.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C07\", \"acip\": \",CI'I PHYIR TSUL KHRIMS GSUM DU BYAS SHE NA, NYAN THOS KYI THEG PA LA NYON MONGS RTZE 'JIL BA'I SO THAR DANG RKYEN YOD KYANG RE ZHIG MI LDANG BA'I BSAM GTAN GYI SDOM PA DANG , RGYUN GCOD PA'I ZAG MED KYI SDOM PA GSUM YOD PA DANG GRANGS MTHUN PAR BYAS PA'O ZHES 'GREL PA RNAMS LAS 'CHAD MOD KYANG ,\", \"wylie\": \"ci'i phyir tshul khrims gsum du byas she na, nyan thos kyi theg pa la nyon mongs rtse 'jil ba'i so thar dang rkyen yod kyang re zhig mi ldang ba'i bsam gtan gyi sdom pa dang , rgyun gcod pa'i zag med kyi sdom pa gsum yod pa dang grangs mthun par byas pa'o zhes 'grel pa rnams las 'chad mod kyang\", \"english\": \"\\\"Why is it,\\\" one may ask, \\\"that you make this division into three different types of morality?\\\" Admittedly there are commentaries which explain that this division into three is meant to coincide with the division made in the way of the listeners, where the vows are described as three: 1) The vows of individual freedom, which function to take the soften the mental afflictions; 2) The vows of meditation, where given the right circumstances the mental afflictions could still arise but don't for the time being, since one is in a state of meditation; and 3) The unstained vows, where the mental afflictions are discontinued completely.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 2535,
        "course": "C08",
        "seq": 190,
        "acip": "[III.157-8a] REG PA'I ZAS LA SOGS PA DE DAG GI NGO BO GANG ZHE NA, REG PA DANG SEMS PA DANG RNAM PAR SHES PA NI ZAG PA DANG BCAS PA ZAS YIN GYI, ZAG MED ZAS MIN TE, ZAS KYI DON NI SRID PA GSO BAR BYED PA YIN NA, ZAG MED NI SRID PA ZAD BYED YIN PA'I PHYIR DANG , MDO LAS, SEMS CAN BYUNG BA RNAMS GNAS PAR BYED PA DANG , 'TSO BAR BYED PA DANG , SRID PA TSOL BA RNAMS LA PHAN 'DOGS PAR BYED PA'O, , ZHES DANG ,",
        "wylie": "[iii.157-8a] reg pa'i zas la sogs pa de dag gi ngo bo gang zhe na, reg pa dang sems pa dang rnam par shes pa ni zag pa dang bcas pa zas yin gyi, zag med zas min te, zas kyi don ni srid pa gso bar byed pa yin na, zag med ni srid pa zad byed yin pa'i phyir dang , mdo las, sems can byung ba rnams gnas par byed pa dang , 'tsho bar byed pa dang , srid pa tshol ba rnams la phan 'dogs par byed pa'o, , zhes dang",
        "english": "\"You have mentioned,\" one may begin, \"sustenance consisting of contact, and so on. Just what is the nature of each of these types of sustenance?\" Contact, mental movement, and consciousness that come with stain are each a kind of sustenance. In their unstained form they can't be called sustenance, for sustenance is defined by its function of perpetuating this suffering life, whereas the stainless act to bring it to a stop. As sutra says, It functions to keep alive those beings who have already come out, and to nourish them; it functions to provide aid to those still in the state of seeking their next life.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C08\", \"acip\": \"[III.157-8a] REG PA'I ZAS LA SOGS PA DE DAG GI NGO BO GANG ZHE NA, REG PA DANG SEMS PA DANG RNAM PAR SHES PA NI ZAG PA DANG BCAS PA ZAS YIN GYI, ZAG MED ZAS MIN TE, ZAS KYI DON NI SRID PA GSO BAR BYED PA YIN NA, ZAG MED NI SRID PA ZAD BYED YIN PA'I PHYIR DANG , MDO LAS, SEMS CAN BYUNG BA RNAMS GNAS PAR BYED PA DANG , 'TSO BAR BYED PA DANG , SRID PA TSOL BA RNAMS LA PHAN 'DOGS PAR BYED PA'O, , ZHES DANG ,\", \"wylie\": \"[iii.157-8a] reg pa'i zas la sogs pa de dag gi ngo bo gang zhe na, reg pa dang sems pa dang rnam par shes pa ni zag pa dang bcas pa zas yin gyi, zag med zas min te, zas kyi don ni srid pa gso bar byed pa yin na, zag med ni srid pa zad byed yin pa'i phyir dang , mdo las, sems can byung ba rnams gnas par byed pa dang , 'tsho bar byed pa dang , srid pa tshol ba rnams la phan 'dogs par byed pa'o, , zhes dang\", \"english\": \"\\\"You have mentioned,\\\" one may begin, \\\"sustenance consisting of contact, and so on. Just what is the nature of each of these types of sustenance?\\\" Contact, mental movement, and consciousness that come with stain are each a kind of sustenance. In their unstained form they can't be called sustenance, for sustenance is defined by its function of perpetuating this suffering life, whereas the stainless act to bring it to a stop. As sutra says, It functions to keep alive those beings who have already come out, and to nourish them; it functions to provide aid to those still in the state of seeking their next life.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 2536,
        "course": "C08",
        "seq": 191,
        "acip": "ZAS BZHI NI NAD DANG 'BRAS DANG ZUG RNGU'I RTZA BA YIN NO, , RGA SHI'I RKYEN YIN NO, , ZHES GSUNGS LA, ZAG MED DE LTA MIN PA'I PHYIR,",
        "wylie": "zas bzhi ni nad dang 'bras dang zug rngu'i rtsa ba yin no, , rga shi'i rkyen yin no, , zhes gsungs la, zag med de lta min pa'i phyir",
        "english": "It also states, The four types of sustenance are the very root of sickness, cancer, agony. They help bring about old age and death. Stainless things could hardly be described this way.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C08\", \"acip\": \"ZAS BZHI NI NAD DANG 'BRAS DANG ZUG RNGU'I RTZA BA YIN NO, , RGA SHI'I RKYEN YIN NO, , ZHES GSUNGS LA, ZAG MED DE LTA MIN PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"zas bzhi ni nad dang 'bras dang zug rngu'i rtsa ba yin no, , rga shi'i rkyen yin no, , zhes gsungs la, zag med de lta min pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"It also states, The four types of sustenance are the very root of sickness, cancer, agony. They help bring about old age and death. Stainless things could hardly be described this way.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 6178,
        "course": "C16",
        "seq": 160,
        "acip": "DE YANG SEMS CAN 'DI DAG LA ZAG MED KYI BDE BA TA GA LA YOD DE, ZAG BCAS KYI BDE BA TZAM YANG MI 'DUG, SEMS CAN KHO RANG TSOS BDE BA YIN SNYAM PA 'DI RNAMS KYANG NGO BO 'GYUR BA'I SDUG BSNGAL SHA STAG YIN, 'DOD RGYU BDE BA 'DOD KYANG BDE BA'I RGYUR DGE BA SGRUB MI SHES, MI 'DOD RGYU SDUG BSNGAL MI 'DOD KYANG , SDUG BSNGAL GYI RGYU SDIG PA SPONG MI SHES, BLANG DOR GO LOG TU SPYAD DE SDUG BSNGAL GYIS NYAM THAG PA'I MA RGAN SEMS CAN",
        "wylie": "de yang sems can 'di dag la zag med kyi bde ba ta ga la yod de, zag bcas kyi bde ba tsam yang mi 'dug, sems can kho rang tshos bde ba yin snyam pa 'di rnams kyang ngo bo 'gyur ba'i sdug bsngal sha stag yin, 'dod rgyu bde ba 'dod kyang bde ba'i rgyur dge ba sgrub mi shes, mi 'dod rgyu sdug bsngal mi 'dod kyang , sdug bsngal gyi rgyu sdig pa spong mi shes, blang dor go log tu spyad de sdug bsngal gyis nyam thag pa'i ma rgan sems can",
        "english": "Right now it's absurd to say that these beings have any kind of pure happiness--they don't even have any of the impure kinds. Every single thing they think is happiness is, in its essence, nothing more than pain. They want wantables but don't want to know about doing the good deeds that bring happiness. They want no unwantables but don't want to know about giving up the bad deeds that bring pain. They act ass backwards: they do what they shouldn't and don't what they should. And so my dear aged mothers, these living beings, are made to suffer.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \"DE YANG SEMS CAN 'DI DAG LA ZAG MED KYI BDE BA TA GA LA YOD DE, ZAG BCAS KYI BDE BA TZAM YANG MI 'DUG, SEMS CAN KHO RANG TSOS BDE BA YIN SNYAM PA 'DI RNAMS KYANG NGO BO 'GYUR BA'I SDUG BSNGAL SHA STAG YIN, 'DOD RGYU BDE BA 'DOD KYANG BDE BA'I RGYUR DGE BA SGRUB MI SHES, MI 'DOD RGYU SDUG BSNGAL MI 'DOD KYANG , SDUG BSNGAL GYI RGYU SDIG PA SPONG MI SHES, BLANG DOR GO LOG TU SPYAD DE SDUG BSNGAL GYIS NYAM THAG PA'I MA RGAN SEMS CAN\", \"wylie\": \"de yang sems can 'di dag la zag med kyi bde ba ta ga la yod de, zag bcas kyi bde ba tsam yang mi 'dug, sems can kho rang tshos bde ba yin snyam pa 'di rnams kyang ngo bo 'gyur ba'i sdug bsngal sha stag yin, 'dod rgyu bde ba 'dod kyang bde ba'i rgyur dge ba sgrub mi shes, mi 'dod rgyu sdug bsngal mi 'dod kyang , sdug bsngal gyi rgyu sdig pa spong mi shes, blang dor go log tu spyad de sdug bsngal gyis nyam thag pa'i ma rgan sems can\", \"english\": \"Right now it's absurd to say that these beings have any kind of pure happiness--they don't even have any of the impure kinds. Every single thing they think is happiness is, in its essence, nothing more than pain. They want wantables but don't want to know about doing the good deeds that bring happiness. They want no unwantables but don't want to know about giving up the bad deeds that bring pain. They act ass backwards: they do what they shouldn't and don't what they should. And so my dear aged mothers, these living beings, are made to suffer.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 6705,
        "course": "C16",
        "seq": 687,
        "acip": "SLOB DPON GANG SPEL NI, ZAG BCAS ZAG PA MED CHOS RNAMS, , ZHES PA MDOR BSTAN DU BYAS NAS DE NYID RGYAS PAR 'CHAD PA LA GNAS BRGYAD DU MDZAD DE, DE LA ZAG BCAS ZAG MED SPYIR BSTAN PA'I PHYIR GNAS DANG PO GNYIS BSTAN, ZAG BCAS BYE BRAG TU STON PA'I DBANG DU BYAS NAS GNAS GSUM STE, GANG KUN NAS NYON MONGS PA, GANG DU KUN NAS NYON MONGS PA, JI LTAR KUN NAS NYON MONGS PA GSUM RIM PA LTAR, SEMS CAN GYI 'JIG RTEN KHAMS GSUM 'GRO BA LNGA DANG , SNOD KYI 'JIG RTEN DANG , SKYE GNAS BZHI RTEN 'BREL YAN LAG BCU GNYIS STON PA GNAS GSUM PA DANG , GANG GIS KUN NAS NYON MONGS PAR BYED PA'I ZAG BCAS KYI LAS DANG NYON MONGS PA'I DBANG DU MDZAD NAS, GNAS BZHI PA DANG LNGA PA DANG ,",
        "wylie": "slob dpon gang spel ni, zag bcas zag pa med chos rnams, , zhes pa mdor bstan du byas nas de nyid rgyas par 'chad pa la gnas brgyad du mdzad de, de la zag bcas zag med spyir bstan pa'i phyir gnas dang po gnyis bstan, zag bcas bye brag tu ston pa'i dbang du byas nas gnas gsum ste, gang kun nas nyon mongs pa, gang du kun nas nyon mongs pa, ji ltar kun nas nyon mongs pa gsum rim pa ltar, sems can gyi 'jig rten khams gsum 'gro ba lnga dang , snod kyi 'jig rten dang , skye gnas bzhi rten 'brel yan lag bcu gnyis ston pa gnas gsum pa dang , gang gis kun nas nyon mongs par byed pa'i zag bcas kyi las dang nyon mongs pa'i dbang du mdzad nas, gnas bzhi pa dang lnga pa dang",
        "english": "Master Purnavardhana presents the eight chapters of the Treasure House as follows: Eight chapters were written to elucidate in detail what is indicated only briefly by the opening words of the texts: \"All phenomena, stained or without stain.\" The first two of the chapters are devoted to a general treatment of stained and unstained phenomena. A detailed presentation of stained phenomena is left to the next three chapters. The third, for example, relates (1) who it is that is so very afflicted, (2) where they live so very afflicted, and (3) how they are so very afflicted. It does so with respective presentations on (1) the five types of beings in the three realms-- the world of living beings; (2) the external world--the \"vessel\" which holds these beings, and (3) the four modes of birth and twelve links of dependent origination. The fourth and fifth chapters describe what it is that makes beings so very afflicted-- stained deeds and the mental afflictions.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \"SLOB DPON GANG SPEL NI, ZAG BCAS ZAG PA MED CHOS RNAMS, , ZHES PA MDOR BSTAN DU BYAS NAS DE NYID RGYAS PAR 'CHAD PA LA GNAS BRGYAD DU MDZAD DE, DE LA ZAG BCAS ZAG MED SPYIR BSTAN PA'I PHYIR GNAS DANG PO GNYIS BSTAN, ZAG BCAS BYE BRAG TU STON PA'I DBANG DU BYAS NAS GNAS GSUM STE, GANG KUN NAS NYON MONGS PA, GANG DU KUN NAS NYON MONGS PA, JI LTAR KUN NAS NYON MONGS PA GSUM RIM PA LTAR, SEMS CAN GYI 'JIG RTEN KHAMS GSUM 'GRO BA LNGA DANG , SNOD KYI 'JIG RTEN DANG , SKYE GNAS BZHI RTEN 'BREL YAN LAG BCU GNYIS STON PA GNAS GSUM PA DANG , GANG GIS KUN NAS NYON MONGS PAR BYED PA'I ZAG BCAS KYI LAS DANG NYON MONGS PA'I DBANG DU MDZAD NAS, GNAS BZHI PA DANG LNGA PA DANG ,\", \"wylie\": \"slob dpon gang spel ni, zag bcas zag pa med chos rnams, , zhes pa mdor bstan du byas nas de nyid rgyas par 'chad pa la gnas brgyad du mdzad de, de la zag bcas zag med spyir bstan pa'i phyir gnas dang po gnyis bstan, zag bcas bye brag tu ston pa'i dbang du byas nas gnas gsum ste, gang kun nas nyon mongs pa, gang du kun nas nyon mongs pa, ji ltar kun nas nyon mongs pa gsum rim pa ltar, sems can gyi 'jig rten khams gsum 'gro ba lnga dang , snod kyi 'jig rten dang , skye gnas bzhi rten 'brel yan lag bcu gnyis ston pa gnas gsum pa dang , gang gis kun nas nyon mongs par byed pa'i zag bcas kyi las dang nyon mongs pa'i dbang du mdzad nas, gnas bzhi pa dang lnga pa dang\", \"english\": \"Master Purnavardhana presents the eight chapters of the Treasure House as follows: Eight chapters were written to elucidate in detail what is indicated only briefly by the opening words of the texts: \\\"All phenomena, stained or without stain.\\\" The first two of the chapters are devoted to a general treatment of stained and unstained phenomena. A detailed presentation of stained phenomena is left to the next three chapters. The third, for example, relates (1) who it is that is so very afflicted, (2) where they live so very afflicted, and (3) how they are so very afflicted. It does so with respective presentations on (1) the five types of beings in the three realms-- the world of living beings; (2) the external world--the \\\"vessel\\\" which holds these beings, and (3) the four modes of birth and twelve links of dependent origination. The fourth and fifth chapters describe what it is that makes beings so very afflicted-- stained deeds and the mental afflictions.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 6706,
        "course": "C16",
        "seq": 688,
        "acip": "ZAG MED BYE BRAG TU STON PA'I DBANG DU MDZAD NAS GNAS PHYI MA GSUM STE, GANG RNAM PAR BYANG BA'I GANG ZAG DANG GANG DU RNAM PAR BYANG BA'I GNAS DANG JI LTAR RNAM PAR BYANG BA'I MNGON PAR RTOGS PA'I RIM PA STON PA BNAS DRUG PA DANG , MDO LAS, NYON MONGS PA DAG NI YE SHES KYIS GZHOM PAR BYA BA YIN NO, , ZHES 'BYUNG BAS GANG GIS RNAM PAR BYANG BAR BYED PA GNAS BDUN PA DANG , MDO LAS, SEMS MNYAM PAR BZHAG PAS NI YANG DAG PA JI LTA BA BZHIN DU RAB TU SHES SO, , ZHES 'BYUNG BAS YE SHES RTEN BRTEN PA'I DBANG DU MDZAD NAS GNAS BRGYAD PA SNYOMS 'JUG BSTAN PA BSHAD DO ZHES 'CHAD DO,",
        "wylie": "zag med bye brag tu ston pa'i dbang du mdzad nas gnas phyi ma gsum ste, gang rnam par byang ba'i gang zag dang gang du rnam par byang ba'i gnas dang ji ltar rnam par byang ba'i mngon par rtogs pa'i rim pa ston pa bnas drug pa dang , mdo las, nyon mongs pa dag ni ye shes kyis gzhom par bya ba yin no, , zhes 'byung bas gang gis rnam par byang bar byed pa gnas bdun pa dang , mdo las, sems mnyam par bzhag pas ni yang dag pa ji lta ba bzhin du rab tu shes so, , zhes 'byung bas ye shes rten brten pa'i dbang du mdzad nas gnas brgyad pa snyoms 'jug bstan pa bshad do zhes 'chad do",
        "english": "The final three chapters give a detailed treatment of unstained phenomena. Chapter Six covers who it is, what kind of person, that is purified; where it is, the place, that he is purified; and how it is, by what stages of realization, that he is purified. Chapter Seven concerns what it is that makes the person pure: as sutra says, \"Affliction is something wisdom must destroy.\" Chapter Eight concerns meditation--that which provides a base for wisdom to rely on--for as sutra states again, \"The mind in meditation gleans pure reality.\"",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \"ZAG MED BYE BRAG TU STON PA'I DBANG DU MDZAD NAS GNAS PHYI MA GSUM STE, GANG RNAM PAR BYANG BA'I GANG ZAG DANG GANG DU RNAM PAR BYANG BA'I GNAS DANG JI LTAR RNAM PAR BYANG BA'I MNGON PAR RTOGS PA'I RIM PA STON PA BNAS DRUG PA DANG , MDO LAS, NYON MONGS PA DAG NI YE SHES KYIS GZHOM PAR BYA BA YIN NO, , ZHES 'BYUNG BAS GANG GIS RNAM PAR BYANG BAR BYED PA GNAS BDUN PA DANG , MDO LAS, SEMS MNYAM PAR BZHAG PAS NI YANG DAG PA JI LTA BA BZHIN DU RAB TU SHES SO, , ZHES 'BYUNG BAS YE SHES RTEN BRTEN PA'I DBANG DU MDZAD NAS GNAS BRGYAD PA SNYOMS 'JUG BSTAN PA BSHAD DO ZHES 'CHAD DO,\", \"wylie\": \"zag med bye brag tu ston pa'i dbang du mdzad nas gnas phyi ma gsum ste, gang rnam par byang ba'i gang zag dang gang du rnam par byang ba'i gnas dang ji ltar rnam par byang ba'i mngon par rtogs pa'i rim pa ston pa bnas drug pa dang , mdo las, nyon mongs pa dag ni ye shes kyis gzhom par bya ba yin no, , zhes 'byung bas gang gis rnam par byang bar byed pa gnas bdun pa dang , mdo las, sems mnyam par bzhag pas ni yang dag pa ji lta ba bzhin du rab tu shes so, , zhes 'byung bas ye shes rten brten pa'i dbang du mdzad nas gnas brgyad pa snyoms 'jug bstan pa bshad do zhes 'chad do\", \"english\": \"The final three chapters give a detailed treatment of unstained phenomena. Chapter Six covers who it is, what kind of person, that is purified; where it is, the place, that he is purified; and how it is, by what stages of realization, that he is purified. Chapter Seven concerns what it is that makes the person pure: as sutra says, \\\"Affliction is something wisdom must destroy.\\\" Chapter Eight concerns meditation--that which provides a base for wisdom to rely on--for as sutra states again, \\\"The mind in meditation gleans pure reality.\\\"\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 6708,
        "course": "C16",
        "seq": 690,
        "acip": ",GNYIS PA NI, CHOS MNGON PA'I BRJOD BYA ZAG BCAS ZAG MED STON PA'I DBANG DU MDZAD NAS GNAS DANG PO BSHAD DO, , DER MING TZAM SMOS PA'I DBANG PO DANG 'DUS BYAS KYI SKYE TSUL RGYAS PAR BSTAN PA'I PHYIR DE'I RJES LA GNYIS PA DANG , DE DAG TU KHAMS GSUM GYI MING TZAM SMOS PA RGYAS PAR BSHAD PA'I PHYIR DU DE'I RJES LA GNAS GSUM PA DANG , KHAMS GSUM BSTAN PA DE'I RGYU DBANG PHYUG LA SOGS PA YIN PA BKAG NAS DNGOS SU LAS LAS BYUNG NGO ZHES BSTAN PA'I PHYIR DE'I RJES LA GNAS BZHI PA DANG ,",
        "wylie": "gnyis pa ni, chos mngon pa'i brjod bya zag bcas zag med ston pa'i dbang du mdzad nas gnas dang po bshad do, , der ming tsam smos pa'i dbang po dang 'dus byas kyi skye tshul rgyas par bstan pa'i phyir de'i rjes la gnyis pa dang , de dag tu khams gsum gyi ming tsam smos pa rgyas par bshad pa'i phyir du de'i rjes la gnas gsum pa dang , khams gsum bstan pa de'i rgyu dbang phyug la sogs pa yin pa bkag nas dngos su las las byung ngo zhes bstan pa'i phyir de'i rjes la gnas bzhi pa dang",
        "english": "As for the interrelation of these chapters, the first presents the general subject matter addressed by the works on knowledge: stained and unstained phenomena. The first chapter makes but a mere mention of the powers and of the way in which caused phenomena arise; thus the following chapter, the second, is devoted to a more detailed treatment of these two points. The subject of the three realms, given only passing mention in these opening chapters, is therefore explored in the third. Some believe that the three realms thus presented are creations of some god, such as the one they call \"Powerful\"; the following, fourth chapter therefore disproves this idea and shows that the real source is deeds.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \",GNYIS PA NI, CHOS MNGON PA'I BRJOD BYA ZAG BCAS ZAG MED STON PA'I DBANG DU MDZAD NAS GNAS DANG PO BSHAD DO, , DER MING TZAM SMOS PA'I DBANG PO DANG 'DUS BYAS KYI SKYE TSUL RGYAS PAR BSTAN PA'I PHYIR DE'I RJES LA GNYIS PA DANG , DE DAG TU KHAMS GSUM GYI MING TZAM SMOS PA RGYAS PAR BSHAD PA'I PHYIR DU DE'I RJES LA GNAS GSUM PA DANG , KHAMS GSUM BSTAN PA DE'I RGYU DBANG PHYUG LA SOGS PA YIN PA BKAG NAS DNGOS SU LAS LAS BYUNG NGO ZHES BSTAN PA'I PHYIR DE'I RJES LA GNAS BZHI PA DANG ,\", \"wylie\": \"gnyis pa ni, chos mngon pa'i brjod bya zag bcas zag med ston pa'i dbang du mdzad nas gnas dang po bshad do, , der ming tsam smos pa'i dbang po dang 'dus byas kyi skye tshul rgyas par bstan pa'i phyir de'i rjes la gnyis pa dang , de dag tu khams gsum gyi ming tsam smos pa rgyas par bshad pa'i phyir du de'i rjes la gnas gsum pa dang , khams gsum bstan pa de'i rgyu dbang phyug la sogs pa yin pa bkag nas dngos su las las byung ngo zhes bstan pa'i phyir de'i rjes la gnas bzhi pa dang\", \"english\": \"As for the interrelation of these chapters, the first presents the general subject matter addressed by the works on knowledge: stained and unstained phenomena. The first chapter makes but a mere mention of the powers and of the way in which caused phenomena arise; thus the following chapter, the second, is devoted to a more detailed treatment of these two points. The subject of the three realms, given only passing mention in these opening chapters, is therefore explored in the third. Some believe that the three realms thus presented are creations of some god, such as the one they call \\\"Powerful\\\"; the following, fourth chapter therefore disproves this idea and shows that the real source is deeds.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 6714,
        "course": "C16",
        "seq": 696,
        "acip": "ZAG MED KYI MTHONG SGOM MI SLOB LAM GSUM PO CHOS CAN, DON DAM PA'I CHOS MNGON PA YIN TE, DRI MA MED PA'I SHES RAB RJES 'BRANG DANG BCAS PA YIN PA'I PHYIR,",
        "wylie": "zag med kyi mthong sgom mi slob lam gsum po chos can, don dam pa'i chos mngon pa yin te, dri ma med pa'i shes rab rjes 'brang dang bcas pa yin pa'i phyir",
        "english": "Consider the following three paths without stain: those of seeing, habituation, and no further learning. They are actual knowledge, because They constitute unstained wisdom and its accessories.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \"ZAG MED KYI MTHONG SGOM MI SLOB LAM GSUM PO CHOS CAN, DON DAM PA'I CHOS MNGON PA YIN TE, DRI MA MED PA'I SHES RAB RJES 'BRANG DANG BCAS PA YIN PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"zag med kyi mthong sgom mi slob lam gsum po chos can, don dam pa'i chos mngon pa yin te, dri ma med pa'i shes rab rjes 'brang dang bcas pa yin pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Consider the following three paths without stain: those of seeing, habituation, and no further learning. They are actual knowledge, because They constitute unstained wisdom and its accessories.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 6719,
        "course": "C16",
        "seq": 701,
        "acip": ",DE RNAMS CHOS CAN, KHYOD LA CHOS MNGON PA'I MING GIS 'DOGS PA'I RGYU MTSAN YOD DE, KHYOD DON DAM PA'I CHOS MNGON PA THOB PA'I THABS SAM RGYU YIN PA'I PHYIR, DE YANG RGYUR 'GYUR TSUL NI SKYES THOB KYI SHES RAB LA BRTEN NAS, THOS BYUNG , DE LAS BSAM BYUNG , DE LAS SGOM BYUNG , DE LAS ZAG MED KYI SHES RAB SKYE BA'I PHYIR,",
        "wylie": "de rnams chos can, khyod la chos mngon pa'i ming gis 'dogs pa'i rgyu mtshan yod de, khyod don dam pa'i chos mngon pa thob pa'i thabs sam rgyu yin pa'i phyir, de yang rgyur 'gyur tshul ni skyes thob kyi shes rab la brten nas, thos byung , de las bsam byung , de las sgom byung , de las zag med kyi shes rab skye ba'i phyir",
        "english": "Let us use the form of a logical statement: Consider the accessories to knowledge just mentioned. They may be given the name \"knowledge,\" because They constitute the means or cause of one's achieving actual knowledge.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \",DE RNAMS CHOS CAN, KHYOD LA CHOS MNGON PA'I MING GIS 'DOGS PA'I RGYU MTSAN YOD DE, KHYOD DON DAM PA'I CHOS MNGON PA THOB PA'I THABS SAM RGYU YIN PA'I PHYIR, DE YANG RGYUR 'GYUR TSUL NI SKYES THOB KYI SHES RAB LA BRTEN NAS, THOS BYUNG , DE LAS BSAM BYUNG , DE LAS SGOM BYUNG , DE LAS ZAG MED KYI SHES RAB SKYE BA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"de rnams chos can, khyod la chos mngon pa'i ming gis 'dogs pa'i rgyu mtshan yod de, khyod don dam pa'i chos mngon pa thob pa'i thabs sam rgyu yin pa'i phyir, de yang rgyur 'gyur tshul ni skyes thob kyi shes rab la brten nas, thos byung , de las bsam byung , de las sgom byung , de las zag med kyi shes rab skye ba'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Let us use the form of a logical statement: Consider the accessories to knowledge just mentioned. They may be given the name \\\"knowledge,\\\" because They constitute the means or cause of one's achieving actual knowledge.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 6730,
        "course": "C16",
        "seq": 712,
        "acip": "ZAG BCAS ZAG MED KYI CHOS RNAMS BSTAN BCOS KYI BRJOD BYA YIN ZHING DE NI CHOS RNAMS ZHES PAS BSTAN, DE LA BRTEN NAS SLOB MA'I RGYUD LA CHOS RAB TU RNAM 'BYED KYI SHES RAB BSKYED PA NI DGOS PA YIN ZHING DE NI RAB TU RNAM 'BYED CES PAS BSTAN, DE LA BRTEN NAS LHAG BCAS LHAG MED KYI MYANG 'DAS THOB PA NYID DGOS YIN ZHING DE NI, MED PAR NYON MONGS RNAMS ZHES PA NAS, DE BAS ZHES PA'I BAR GYIS SHUGS LA BSTAN NYING DGOS DGOS PA, DGOS PA BSTAN BCOS KYI BRJOD BYA LA RAG LAS PA NI 'BREL PA'O, , YANG NA CHOS RNAMS BRJOD BYA DANG , BSTAN BCOS 'DI RJOD BYED DU 'BREL PA NI 'BREL PA'O,",
        "wylie": "zag bcas zag med kyi chos rnams bstan bcos kyi brjod bya yin zhing de ni chos rnams zhes pas bstan, de la brten nas slob ma'i rgyud la chos rab tu rnam 'byed kyi shes rab bskyed pa ni dgos pa yin zhing de ni rab tu rnam 'byed ces pas bstan, de la brten nas lhag bcas lhag med kyi myang 'das thob pa nyid dgos yin zhing de ni, med par nyon mongs rnams zhes pa nas, de bas zhes pa'i bar gyis shugs la bstan nying dgos dgos pa, dgos pa bstan bcos kyi brjod bya la rag las pa ni 'brel pa'o, , yang na chos rnams brjod bya dang , bstan bcos 'di rjod byed du 'brel pa ni 'brel pa'o",
        "english": "Here are the four attributes: 1) The subject matter of the text concerns stained and unstained types of phenomena. It is indicated in the verse with the words \"every existing phenomenon.\" 2) The purpose is to utilize this subject matter to plant, in students' minds, that wisdom which analyzes phenomena in an ultimate way. It is indicated by the words \"ultimate analysis.\" 3) The ultimate goal is to have these students achieve nirvana, both with and without anything remaining. It is indicated indirectly by the entire phrase running from \"There's no way...\" up to \"...without,\" and on from \"this affliction...\" up to the word \"Thus.\" 4) A relation exists in that the purpose must be achieved through the subject matter of the work, and the ultimate goal through this very purpose. One may also state the relation as being between the subject matter of the work (that is, all existing phenomena) and the means by which this subject matter is expressed (i.e., the commentary itself). 10 What Causes Worlds",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \"ZAG BCAS ZAG MED KYI CHOS RNAMS BSTAN BCOS KYI BRJOD BYA YIN ZHING DE NI CHOS RNAMS ZHES PAS BSTAN, DE LA BRTEN NAS SLOB MA'I RGYUD LA CHOS RAB TU RNAM 'BYED KYI SHES RAB BSKYED PA NI DGOS PA YIN ZHING DE NI RAB TU RNAM 'BYED CES PAS BSTAN, DE LA BRTEN NAS LHAG BCAS LHAG MED KYI MYANG 'DAS THOB PA NYID DGOS YIN ZHING DE NI, MED PAR NYON MONGS RNAMS ZHES PA NAS, DE BAS ZHES PA'I BAR GYIS SHUGS LA BSTAN NYING DGOS DGOS PA, DGOS PA BSTAN BCOS KYI BRJOD BYA LA RAG LAS PA NI 'BREL PA'O, , YANG NA CHOS RNAMS BRJOD BYA DANG , BSTAN BCOS 'DI RJOD BYED DU 'BREL PA NI 'BREL PA'O,\", \"wylie\": \"zag bcas zag med kyi chos rnams bstan bcos kyi brjod bya yin zhing de ni chos rnams zhes pas bstan, de la brten nas slob ma'i rgyud la chos rab tu rnam 'byed kyi shes rab bskyed pa ni dgos pa yin zhing de ni rab tu rnam 'byed ces pas bstan, de la brten nas lhag bcas lhag med kyi myang 'das thob pa nyid dgos yin zhing de ni, med par nyon mongs rnams zhes pa nas, de bas zhes pa'i bar gyis shugs la bstan nying dgos dgos pa, dgos pa bstan bcos kyi brjod bya la rag las pa ni 'brel pa'o, , yang na chos rnams brjod bya dang , bstan bcos 'di rjod byed du 'brel pa ni 'brel pa'o\", \"english\": \"Here are the four attributes: 1) The subject matter of the text concerns stained and unstained types of phenomena. It is indicated in the verse with the words \\\"every existing phenomenon.\\\" 2) The purpose is to utilize this subject matter to plant, in students' minds, that wisdom which analyzes phenomena in an ultimate way. It is indicated by the words \\\"ultimate analysis.\\\" 3) The ultimate goal is to have these students achieve nirvana, both with and without anything remaining. It is indicated indirectly by the entire phrase running from \\\"There's no way...\\\" up to \\\"...without,\\\" and on from \\\"this affliction...\\\" up to the word \\\"Thus.\\\" 4) A relation exists in that the purpose must be achieved through the subject matter of the work, and the ultimate goal through this very purpose. One may also state the relation as being between the subject matter of the work (that is, all existing phenomena) and the means by which this subject matter is expressed (i.e., the commentary itself). 10 What Causes Worlds\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 6814,
        "course": "C16",
        "seq": 796,
        "acip": ",'O NA MTHONG LAM BAR CHAD MED LAM GYI DUS SU DE'I SPANG BYA MTHONG SPANG MED MOD KYANG , SGOM SPANG GI BAG LA NYAL YOD DGOS LA, DE'I TSE YID KYI SHES PA NI GNYIS SNANG 'KHRUL PA'I BAG CHAGS KYIS MA BSLAD PA'I ZAG MED YIN PAS, DE'I NGO BOR BAG LA NYAL DE GNAS PA MI RIGS LA, DBANG SHES KYANG DE'I RTEN DU MED CING GZUGS KYANG DE'I RTEN DU MI RIGS LA KUN GZHI YANG MI 'DOD PAS DE'I RTEN MED PAR 'GYUR RO SNYAM NA, SKYON MED DE DE'I TSE NGA TZAM ZHIG SGOM SPANG GI BAG LA NYAL GYI RTEN YIN PA'I PHYIR TE, SPANG GNYEN GZHAN LA YANG DE BZHIN DU SHES PAR BYA'O,",
        "wylie": "'o na mthong lam bar chad med lam gyi dus su de'i spang bya mthong spang med mod kyang , sgom spang gi bag la nyal yod dgos la, de'i tshe yid kyi shes pa ni gnyis snang 'khrul pa'i bag chags kyis ma bslad pa'i zag med yin pas, de'i ngo bor bag la nyal de gnas pa mi rigs la, dbang shes kyang de'i rten du med cing gzugs kyang de'i rten du mi rigs la kun gzhi yang mi 'dod pas de'i rten med par 'gyur ro snyam na, skyon med de de'i tshe nga tsam zhig sgom spang gi bag la nyal gyi rten yin pa'i phyir te, spang gnyen gzhan la yang de bzhin du shes par bya'o",
        "english": "One might wonder about something else: Let's talk about the period while you are in the \"uninterrupted\" stage of the path of seeing [the actual direct perception of emptiness]. I can accept that at this point the negative thoughts eliminated by this path are no longer present, but we would have to say that the negative things eliminated by the path of habituation are still there, in a dormant way. At this particular point in time, the consciousness of the mind is unstained, unaffected by the mental seed which causes the mistaken state of mind where the appearance and actuality of things are different from one another. As such none of these things could lie dormant here, due to its very quality at the time. There is no consciousness of the senses that could act as the basis for those mental seeds, and it would be improper to say that physical form could ever provide such a basis either. According to you, moreover, there's no foundation consciousness. I would have to think then that there is no basis at all where these dormant things could stay. And yet there is no such problem, for at this point in time it is the simple \"me\" which is providing the basis for the dormant things eliminated by the path of habituation to stay. You can apply this reasoning as well to all the other cases involving things to be eliminated, and the antidotes which eliminate them. 24 The Middle-Way School on the Question of Where the Seeds of Karma Stay until They Give their Result",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \",'O NA MTHONG LAM BAR CHAD MED LAM GYI DUS SU DE'I SPANG BYA MTHONG SPANG MED MOD KYANG , SGOM SPANG GI BAG LA NYAL YOD DGOS LA, DE'I TSE YID KYI SHES PA NI GNYIS SNANG 'KHRUL PA'I BAG CHAGS KYIS MA BSLAD PA'I ZAG MED YIN PAS, DE'I NGO BOR BAG LA NYAL DE GNAS PA MI RIGS LA, DBANG SHES KYANG DE'I RTEN DU MED CING GZUGS KYANG DE'I RTEN DU MI RIGS LA KUN GZHI YANG MI 'DOD PAS DE'I RTEN MED PAR 'GYUR RO SNYAM NA, SKYON MED DE DE'I TSE NGA TZAM ZHIG SGOM SPANG GI BAG LA NYAL GYI RTEN YIN PA'I PHYIR TE, SPANG GNYEN GZHAN LA YANG DE BZHIN DU SHES PAR BYA'O,\", \"wylie\": \"'o na mthong lam bar chad med lam gyi dus su de'i spang bya mthong spang med mod kyang , sgom spang gi bag la nyal yod dgos la, de'i tshe yid kyi shes pa ni gnyis snang 'khrul pa'i bag chags kyis ma bslad pa'i zag med yin pas, de'i ngo bor bag la nyal de gnas pa mi rigs la, dbang shes kyang de'i rten du med cing gzugs kyang de'i rten du mi rigs la kun gzhi yang mi 'dod pas de'i rten med par 'gyur ro snyam na, skyon med de de'i tshe nga tsam zhig sgom spang gi bag la nyal gyi rten yin pa'i phyir te, spang gnyen gzhan la yang de bzhin du shes par bya'o\", \"english\": \"One might wonder about something else: Let's talk about the period while you are in the \\\"uninterrupted\\\" stage of the path of seeing [the actual direct perception of emptiness]. I can accept that at this point the negative thoughts eliminated by this path are no longer present, but we would have to say that the negative things eliminated by the path of habituation are still there, in a dormant way. At this particular point in time, the consciousness of the mind is unstained, unaffected by the mental seed which causes the mistaken state of mind where the appearance and actuality of things are different from one another. As such none of these things could lie dormant here, due to its very quality at the time. There is no consciousness of the senses that could act as the basis for those mental seeds, and it would be improper to say that physical form could ever provide such a basis either. According to you, moreover, there's no foundation consciousness. I would have to think then that there is no basis at all where these dormant things could stay. And yet there is no such problem, for at this point in time it is the simple \\\"me\\\" which is providing the basis for the dormant things eliminated by the path of habituation to stay. You can apply this reasoning as well to all the other cases involving things to be eliminated, and the antidotes which eliminate them. 24 The Middle-Way School on the Question of Where the Seeds of Karma Stay until They Give their Result\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 6918,
        "course": "C16",
        "seq": 900,
        "acip": ",BAG MED DANG BSKAL BA MI MNYAM PAS GCOD PA MIN ZER YANG , SKAL BA MNYAM MI MNYAM ZAG BCAS ZAG MED LA DMIGS PA KUN GYIS GCOD PAR BYED,",
        "wylie": "bag med dang bskal ba mi mnyam pas gcod pa min zer yang , skal ba mnyam mi mnyam zag bcas zag med la dmigs pa kun gyis gcod par byed",
        "english": "Now some make the claim that views involving unstained phenomena or levels which are not comparable cannot cause a person to lose his most basic virtues. The fact though is that this virtue can be lost through all the forms of this view: through those that focus on levels that are comparable or those which are not; through those that focus on unstained phenomena or on those with stain.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \",BAG MED DANG BSKAL BA MI MNYAM PAS GCOD PA MIN ZER YANG , SKAL BA MNYAM MI MNYAM ZAG BCAS ZAG MED LA DMIGS PA KUN GYIS GCOD PAR BYED,\", \"wylie\": \"bag med dang bskal ba mi mnyam pas gcod pa min zer yang , skal ba mnyam mi mnyam zag bcas zag med la dmigs pa kun gyis gcod par byed\", \"english\": \"Now some make the claim that views involving unstained phenomena or levels which are not comparable cannot cause a person to lose his most basic virtues. The fact though is that this virtue can be lost through all the forms of this view: through those that focus on levels that are comparable or those which are not; through those that focus on unstained phenomena or on those with stain.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 7153,
        "course": "C17",
        "seq": 115,
        "acip": "DE YANG SEMS CAN 'DI DAG LA ZAG MED KYI BDE BA TA GA LA YOD DE, ZAG BCAS KYI BDE BA TZAM YANG MI 'DUG, SEMS CAN KHO RANG TSOS BDE BA YIN SNYAM PA 'DI RNAMS KYANG NGO BO 'GYUR BA'I SDUG BSNGAL SHA STAG YIN, 'DOD RGYU BDE BA 'DOD KYANG BDE BA'I RGYUR DGE BA SGRUB MI SHES, MI 'DOD RGYU SDUG BSNGAL MI 'DOD KYANG , SDUG BSNGAL GYI RGYU SDIG PA SPONG MI SHES, BLANG DOR GO LOG TU SPYAD DE SDUG BSNGAL GYIS NYAM THAG PA'I MA RGAN SEMS CAN 'DI DAG THAMS CAD BDE BA DANG BDE BA'I RGYU THAMS CAD DANG LDAN NA CI MA RUNG , LDAN PAR GYUR CIG, LDAN PA ZHIG NGA RANG GIS BYED, SEMS CAN THAMS CAD SDUG BSNGAL DANG SDUG BSNGAL GYI RGYU THAMS CAD DANG BRAL NA CI MA RUNG , BRAL BAR GYUR CIG, BRAL BA ZHIG NGA RANG GIS BYED SNYAM DU YANG YANG BSGOMS PAS BYAMS PA DANG SNYING RJE SHUGS CAN BSKYED,",
        "wylie": "de yang sems can 'di dag la zag med kyi bde ba ta ga la yod de, zag bcas kyi bde ba tsam yang mi 'dug, sems can kho rang tshos bde ba yin snyam pa 'di rnams kyang ngo bo 'gyur ba'i sdug bsngal sha stag yin, 'dod rgyu bde ba 'dod kyang bde ba'i rgyur dge ba sgrub mi shes, mi 'dod rgyu sdug bsngal mi 'dod kyang , sdug bsngal gyi rgyu sdig pa spong mi shes, blang dor go log tu spyad de sdug bsngal gyis nyam thag pa'i ma rgan sems can 'di dag thams cad bde ba dang bde ba'i rgyu thams cad dang ldan na ci ma rung , ldan par gyur cig, ldan pa zhig nga rang gis byed, sems can thams cad sdug bsngal dang sdug bsngal gyi rgyu thams cad dang bral na ci ma rung , bral bar gyur cig, bral ba zhig nga rang gis byed snyam du yang yang bsgoms pas byams pa dang snying rje shugs can bskyed",
        "english": "Right now it's absurd to say that these beings have any kind of pure happiness--they don't even have any of the impure kinds. Every single thing they think is happiness is, in its essence, nothing more than pain. They want wantables but don't want to know about doing the good deeds that bring happiness. They want no unwantables but don't want to know about giving up the bad deeds that bring pain. They act ass backwards: they do what they shouldn't and don't what they should. And so my dear aged mothers, these living beings, are made to suffer. \"How good it would be if they could all find every happiness, and every cause of happiness. I wish they could. I'll see that they do.\" \"How good it would be if they could all be free of every pain, and every cause of pain. I wish they could. I'll see that they do.\" Let these two trains of thought run through your mind; meditate on them over and over again. Then you will come to feel the very strongest loving-kindness and compassion. 6 Taking Responsibility Yourself",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C17\", \"acip\": \"DE YANG SEMS CAN 'DI DAG LA ZAG MED KYI BDE BA TA GA LA YOD DE, ZAG BCAS KYI BDE BA TZAM YANG MI 'DUG, SEMS CAN KHO RANG TSOS BDE BA YIN SNYAM PA 'DI RNAMS KYANG NGO BO 'GYUR BA'I SDUG BSNGAL SHA STAG YIN, 'DOD RGYU BDE BA 'DOD KYANG BDE BA'I RGYUR DGE BA SGRUB MI SHES, MI 'DOD RGYU SDUG BSNGAL MI 'DOD KYANG , SDUG BSNGAL GYI RGYU SDIG PA SPONG MI SHES, BLANG DOR GO LOG TU SPYAD DE SDUG BSNGAL GYIS NYAM THAG PA'I MA RGAN SEMS CAN 'DI DAG THAMS CAD BDE BA DANG BDE BA'I RGYU THAMS CAD DANG LDAN NA CI MA RUNG , LDAN PAR GYUR CIG, LDAN PA ZHIG NGA RANG GIS BYED, SEMS CAN THAMS CAD SDUG BSNGAL DANG SDUG BSNGAL GYI RGYU THAMS CAD DANG BRAL NA CI MA RUNG , BRAL BAR GYUR CIG, BRAL BA ZHIG NGA RANG GIS BYED SNYAM DU YANG YANG BSGOMS PAS BYAMS PA DANG SNYING RJE SHUGS CAN BSKYED,\", \"wylie\": \"de yang sems can 'di dag la zag med kyi bde ba ta ga la yod de, zag bcas kyi bde ba tsam yang mi 'dug, sems can kho rang tshos bde ba yin snyam pa 'di rnams kyang ngo bo 'gyur ba'i sdug bsngal sha stag yin, 'dod rgyu bde ba 'dod kyang bde ba'i rgyur dge ba sgrub mi shes, mi 'dod rgyu sdug bsngal mi 'dod kyang , sdug bsngal gyi rgyu sdig pa spong mi shes, blang dor go log tu spyad de sdug bsngal gyis nyam thag pa'i ma rgan sems can 'di dag thams cad bde ba dang bde ba'i rgyu thams cad dang ldan na ci ma rung , ldan par gyur cig, ldan pa zhig nga rang gis byed, sems can thams cad sdug bsngal dang sdug bsngal gyi rgyu thams cad dang bral na ci ma rung , bral bar gyur cig, bral ba zhig nga rang gis byed snyam du yang yang bsgoms pas byams pa dang snying rje shugs can bskyed\", \"english\": \"Right now it's absurd to say that these beings have any kind of pure happiness--they don't even have any of the impure kinds. Every single thing they think is happiness is, in its essence, nothing more than pain. They want wantables but don't want to know about doing the good deeds that bring happiness. They want no unwantables but don't want to know about giving up the bad deeds that bring pain. They act ass backwards: they do what they shouldn't and don't what they should. And so my dear aged mothers, these living beings, are made to suffer. \\\"How good it would be if they could all find every happiness, and every cause of happiness. I wish they could. I'll see that they do.\\\" \\\"How good it would be if they could all be free of every pain, and every cause of pain. I wish they could. I'll see that they do.\\\" Let these two trains of thought run through your mind; meditate on them over and over again. Then you will come to feel the very strongest loving-kindness and compassion. 6 Taking Responsibility Yourself\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 7382,
        "course": "C17",
        "seq": 344,
        "acip": "[III.157-8a] REG PA'I ZAS LA SOGS PA DE DAG GI NGO BO GANG ZHE NA, REG PA DANG SEMS PA DANG RNAM PAR SHES PA NI ZAG PA DANG BCAS PA ZAS YIN GYI, ZAG MED ZAS MIN TE, ZAS KYI DON NI SRID PA GSO BAR BYED PA YIN NA, ZAG MED NI SRID PA ZAD BYED YIN PA'I PHYIR DANG , MDO LAS, SEMS CAN BYUNG BA RNAMS GNAS PAR BYED PA DANG , 'TSO BAR BYED PA DANG , SRID PA TSOL BA RNAMS LA PHAN 'DOGS PAR BYED PA'O, , ZHES DANG ,",
        "wylie": "[iii.157-8a] reg pa'i zas la sogs pa de dag gi ngo bo gang zhe na, reg pa dang sems pa dang rnam par shes pa ni zag pa dang bcas pa zas yin gyi, zag med zas min te, zas kyi don ni srid pa gso bar byed pa yin na, zag med ni srid pa zad byed yin pa'i phyir dang , mdo las, sems can byung ba rnams gnas par byed pa dang , 'tsho bar byed pa dang , srid pa tshol ba rnams la phan 'dogs par byed pa'o, , zhes dang",
        "english": "\"You have mentioned,\" one may begin, \"sustenance consisting of contact, and so on. Just what is the nature of each of these types of sustenance?\" Contact, mental movement, and consciousness that come with stain are each a kind of sustenance. In their unstained form they can't be called sustenance, for sustenance is defined by its function of perpetuating this suffering life, whereas the stainless act to bring it to a stop. As sutra says, It functions to keep alive those beings who have already come out, and to nourish them; it functions to provide aid to those still in the state of seeking their next life.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C17\", \"acip\": \"[III.157-8a] REG PA'I ZAS LA SOGS PA DE DAG GI NGO BO GANG ZHE NA, REG PA DANG SEMS PA DANG RNAM PAR SHES PA NI ZAG PA DANG BCAS PA ZAS YIN GYI, ZAG MED ZAS MIN TE, ZAS KYI DON NI SRID PA GSO BAR BYED PA YIN NA, ZAG MED NI SRID PA ZAD BYED YIN PA'I PHYIR DANG , MDO LAS, SEMS CAN BYUNG BA RNAMS GNAS PAR BYED PA DANG , 'TSO BAR BYED PA DANG , SRID PA TSOL BA RNAMS LA PHAN 'DOGS PAR BYED PA'O, , ZHES DANG ,\", \"wylie\": \"[iii.157-8a] reg pa'i zas la sogs pa de dag gi ngo bo gang zhe na, reg pa dang sems pa dang rnam par shes pa ni zag pa dang bcas pa zas yin gyi, zag med zas min te, zas kyi don ni srid pa gso bar byed pa yin na, zag med ni srid pa zad byed yin pa'i phyir dang , mdo las, sems can byung ba rnams gnas par byed pa dang , 'tsho bar byed pa dang , srid pa tshol ba rnams la phan 'dogs par byed pa'o, , zhes dang\", \"english\": \"\\\"You have mentioned,\\\" one may begin, \\\"sustenance consisting of contact, and so on. Just what is the nature of each of these types of sustenance?\\\" Contact, mental movement, and consciousness that come with stain are each a kind of sustenance. In their unstained form they can't be called sustenance, for sustenance is defined by its function of perpetuating this suffering life, whereas the stainless act to bring it to a stop. As sutra says, It functions to keep alive those beings who have already come out, and to nourish them; it functions to provide aid to those still in the state of seeking their next life.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 7383,
        "course": "C17",
        "seq": 345,
        "acip": "ZAS BZHI NI NAD DANG 'BRAS DANG ZUG RNGU'I RTZA BA YIN NO, , RGA SHI'I RKYEN YIN NO, , ZHES GSUNGS LA, ZAG MED DE LTA MIN PA'I PHYIR,",
        "wylie": "zas bzhi ni nad dang 'bras dang zug rngu'i rtsa ba yin no, , rga shi'i rkyen yin no, , zhes gsungs la, zag med de lta min pa'i phyir",
        "english": "It also states, The four types of sustenance are the very root of sickness, cancer, agony. They help bring about old age and death. Stainless things could hardly be described this way. 22 The Realms of Hell",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"C17\", \"acip\": \"ZAS BZHI NI NAD DANG 'BRAS DANG ZUG RNGU'I RTZA BA YIN NO, , RGA SHI'I RKYEN YIN NO, , ZHES GSUNGS LA, ZAG MED DE LTA MIN PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"zas bzhi ni nad dang 'bras dang zug rngu'i rtsa ba yin no, , rga shi'i rkyen yin no, , zhes gsungs la, zag med de lta min pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"It also states, The four types of sustenance are the very root of sickness, cancer, agony. They help bring about old age and death. Stainless things could hardly be described this way. 22 The Realms of Hell\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 24248,
        "course": "GIE",
        "seq": 736,
        "acip": "^ ,,ZAG MED RNAM GROL 'BRAS BU'I RIN CHEN MDZOD, ,THAR 'DOD BLO GSAL BYE BA'I DGA' STON DU, ,'BAD MED LHUG POR DBYE BA'I LEGS BSHAD 'DI, ,DAG GSAL PAR GYI RI MOR DRANGS PA'I DGES,",
        "wylie": "^ ,,zag med rnam grol 'bras bu'i rin chen mdzod, ,thar 'dod blo gsal bye ba'i dga' ston du, ,'bad med lhug por dbye ba'i legs bshad 'di, ,dag gsal par gyi ri mor drangs pa'i dges",
        "english": "A great good deed has been done In producing an edition With the clearest of printed pages As a treasure chest of jewels— The fruits of stainless liberation— Presented as a dinner party For millions of clear-minded souls Whose only wish is freedom. And it is those excellent words Contained within this text That have laid the entertainment out In a surfeit effortless.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"GIE\", \"acip\": \"^ ,,ZAG MED RNAM GROL 'BRAS BU'I RIN CHEN MDZOD, ,THAR 'DOD BLO GSAL BYE BA'I DGA' STON DU, ,'BAD MED LHUG POR DBYE BA'I LEGS BSHAD 'DI, ,DAG GSAL PAR GYI RI MOR DRANGS PA'I DGES,\", \"wylie\": \"^ ,,zag med rnam grol 'bras bu'i rin chen mdzod, ,thar 'dod blo gsal bye ba'i dga' ston du, ,'bad med lhug por dbye ba'i legs bshad 'di, ,dag gsal par gyi ri mor drangs pa'i dges\", \"english\": \"A great good deed has been done In producing an edition With the clearest of printed pages As a treasure chest of jewels— The fruits of stainless liberation— Presented as a dinner party For millions of clear-minded souls Whose only wish is freedom. And it is those excellent words Contained within this text That have laid the entertainment out In a surfeit effortless.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 24735,
        "course": "GIE",
        "seq": 1223,
        "acip": "[E128] KHA CIG GZHUNG GI DGONGS PA DE LTAR YIN TE, MTHONG LAM RTZE SBYOR DU GSUNGS PA'I PHYIR DANG, ZAG MED KYI YE SHES KYI SDE TSAN GYI SKABS SU YE SHES SU GSUNGS PA'I PHYIR RO, ZHES ZER BA NI CHES SHIN TU MA NGES TE,",
        "wylie": "[e128] kha cig gzhung gi dgongs pa de ltar yin te, mthong lam rtse sbyor du gsungs pa'i phyir dang, zag med kyi ye shes kyi sde tshan gyi skabs su ye shes su gsungs pa'i phyir ro, zhes zer ba ni ches shin tu ma nges te",
        "english": "Some claim that what the text really means to say is that cessation meditation is a “peak application,” within the path of seeing, and that in the context of the group of different forms of stainless wisdom it was stated to be a kind of wisdom.  This position though is indefinite to the extreme.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"GIE\", \"acip\": \"[E128] KHA CIG GZHUNG GI DGONGS PA DE LTAR YIN TE, MTHONG LAM RTZE SBYOR DU GSUNGS PA'I PHYIR DANG, ZAG MED KYI YE SHES KYI SDE TSAN GYI SKABS SU YE SHES SU GSUNGS PA'I PHYIR RO, ZHES ZER BA NI CHES SHIN TU MA NGES TE,\", \"wylie\": \"[e128] kha cig gzhung gi dgongs pa de ltar yin te, mthong lam rtse sbyor du gsungs pa'i phyir dang, zag med kyi ye shes kyi sde tshan gyi skabs su ye shes su gsungs pa'i phyir ro, zhes zer ba ni ches shin tu ma nges te\", \"english\": \"Some claim that what the text really means to say is that cessation meditation is a “peak application,” within the path of seeing, and that in the context of the group of different forms of stainless wisdom it was stated to be a kind of wisdom.  This position though is indefinite to the extreme.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 24736,
        "course": "GIE",
        "seq": 1224,
        "acip": "[E129] THOD RGAL GYI SPEL CHOS BSHAD PAS DER 'GYUR NA, 'DOD SEMS KYANG DER THAL ZHING, ZAG MED KYI YE SHES KYI SDE TSAN DU BSHAD PAS DER KHAS LEN NA, ZAD PAR GYI SKYE MCHED RNAMS KYANG SPROS PA BKAG PA'I MI RTOG YE SHES LA 'DOD DGOS PAR THAL BA'I PHYIR RO,,",
        "wylie": "[e129] thod rgal gyi spel chos bshad pas der 'gyur na, 'dod sems kyang der thal zhing, zag med kyi ye shes kyi sde tshan du bshad pas der khas len na, zad par gyi skye mched rnams kyang spros pa bkag pa'i mi rtog ye shes la 'dod dgos par thal ba'i phyir ro",
        "english": "In that case, then since this is explained as a form of meditation in which we increase our powers of focus by jumping back and forth to different levels, a desire-realm state of mind could in that case be in a cessation meditation.  And if you accept that just because it was described as a member of the group of different forms of stainless wisdom we should accept that it is thereby a cessation meditation, then you would have to agree as well that those types of concentration where we are able to wrought widespread changes in physical entities would also constitute the form of wisdom which no longer imagines that things could come from their own side, and which thus puts a stop to all false elaborations.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"GIE\", \"acip\": \"[E129] THOD RGAL GYI SPEL CHOS BSHAD PAS DER 'GYUR NA, 'DOD SEMS KYANG DER THAL ZHING, ZAG MED KYI YE SHES KYI SDE TSAN DU BSHAD PAS DER KHAS LEN NA, ZAD PAR GYI SKYE MCHED RNAMS KYANG SPROS PA BKAG PA'I MI RTOG YE SHES LA 'DOD DGOS PAR THAL BA'I PHYIR RO,,\", \"wylie\": \"[e129] thod rgal gyi spel chos bshad pas der 'gyur na, 'dod sems kyang der thal zhing, zag med kyi ye shes kyi sde tshan du bshad pas der khas len na, zad par gyi skye mched rnams kyang spros pa bkag pa'i mi rtog ye shes la 'dod dgos par thal ba'i phyir ro\", \"english\": \"In that case, then since this is explained as a form of meditation in which we increase our powers of focus by jumping back and forth to different levels, a desire-realm state of mind could in that case be in a cessation meditation.  And if you accept that just because it was described as a member of the group of different forms of stainless wisdom we should accept that it is thereby a cessation meditation, then you would have to agree as well that those types of concentration where we are able to wrought widespread changes in physical entities would also constitute the form of wisdom which no longer imagines that things could come from their own side, and which thus puts a stop to all false elaborations.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 27040,
        "course": "GK",
        "seq": 92,
        "acip": ",DE NAS LDOG STE, DE SRID DU ZAG BCAS ZAG MED KYI BAG CHAGS SAM SA BON CI RIGS 'DZIN LA, DE NAS DE'I STENG GI BAG CHAGS 'GAG DGOS THAM {%THAMS} CAD 'GAG ,SAD DGOS PA THAMS CAD SAD PAR 'GYUR BA'I PHYIR RO,,",
        "wylie": "de nas ldog ste, de srid du zag bcas zag med kyi bag chags sam sa bon ci rigs 'dzin la, de nas de'i steng gi bag chags 'gag dgos tham {%thams} cad 'gag ,sad dgos pa thams cad sad par 'gyur ba'i phyir ro",
        "english": "At that point, this consciousness comes to a stop.  Until such time, it retains the karmic propensities, or seeds, whether they be stained or unstained; and then at that time, all the propensities lying in the foundation consciousness which need to stop, stop; and all those that need to open, open. How seeds are imprinted",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"GK\", \"acip\": \",DE NAS LDOG STE, DE SRID DU ZAG BCAS ZAG MED KYI BAG CHAGS SAM SA BON CI RIGS 'DZIN LA, DE NAS DE'I STENG GI BAG CHAGS 'GAG DGOS THAM {%THAMS} CAD 'GAG ,SAD DGOS PA THAMS CAD SAD PAR 'GYUR BA'I PHYIR RO,,\", \"wylie\": \"de nas ldog ste, de srid du zag bcas zag med kyi bag chags sam sa bon ci rigs 'dzin la, de nas de'i steng gi bag chags 'gag dgos tham {%thams} cad 'gag ,sad dgos pa thams cad sad par 'gyur ba'i phyir ro\", \"english\": \"At that point, this consciousness comes to a stop.  Until such time, it retains the karmic propensities, or seeds, whether they be stained or unstained; and then at that time, all the propensities lying in the foundation consciousness which need to stop, stop; and all those that need to open, open. How seeds are imprinted\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 27122,
        "course": "GK",
        "seq": 174,
        "acip": "'DIR MNYAM GZHAG ZAG MED KYI TSE NYON YID DE ZAG MED KHO NAR 'JUG PA'I TSUL GCIG DANG, YANG THEG DMAN MNYAM GZHAG GI TSE CHOS KYI BDAG 'DZIN GYI ZAG PA DANG BCAS PA DANG, THEG CHEN GYI DE'I TSE ZAG MED KHO NAR 'JUG PA'I TSUL TE, LUGS GNYIS DKA' 'GREL LAS GSUNGS PA RNAMS LA ZHIB TU DPYAD PAR BYA'O,,",
        "wylie": "'dir mnyam gzhag zag med kyi tshe nyon yid de zag med kho nar 'jug pa'i tshul gcig dang, yang theg dman mnyam gzhag gi tshe chos kyi bdag 'dzin gyi zag pa dang bcas pa dang, theg chen gyi de'i tshe zag med kho nar 'jug pa'i tshul te, lugs gnyis dka' 'grel las gsungs pa rnams la zhib tu dpyad par bya'o",
        "english": "Now when we are talking here about being in deep meditation which is unstained, there are two ways of looking at it.  According to one, the negative consciousness at this point is engaging only in the unstained.  Another says that—when the deep meditation is being done by a person on the lower way—the negative consciousness still involves the stain of believing that things have some self-nature.  And when the meditation is done by a person on the higher way, then the negative consciousness engages only in the unstained.  These two opinions, which are described in the Commentary on Difficult Points, require a fine analysis. Advice to understand two states of consciousness",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"GK\", \"acip\": \"'DIR MNYAM GZHAG ZAG MED KYI TSE NYON YID DE ZAG MED KHO NAR 'JUG PA'I TSUL GCIG DANG, YANG THEG DMAN MNYAM GZHAG GI TSE CHOS KYI BDAG 'DZIN GYI ZAG PA DANG BCAS PA DANG, THEG CHEN GYI DE'I TSE ZAG MED KHO NAR 'JUG PA'I TSUL TE, LUGS GNYIS DKA' 'GREL LAS GSUNGS PA RNAMS LA ZHIB TU DPYAD PAR BYA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"'dir mnyam gzhag zag med kyi tshe nyon yid de zag med kho nar 'jug pa'i tshul gcig dang, yang theg dman mnyam gzhag gi tshe chos kyi bdag 'dzin gyi zag pa dang bcas pa dang, theg chen gyi de'i tshe zag med kho nar 'jug pa'i tshul te, lugs gnyis dka' 'grel las gsungs pa rnams la zhib tu dpyad par bya'o\", \"english\": \"Now when we are talking here about being in deep meditation which is unstained, there are two ways of looking at it.  According to one, the negative consciousness at this point is engaging only in the unstained.  Another says that—when the deep meditation is being done by a person on the lower way—the negative consciousness still involves the stain of believing that things have some self-nature.  And when the meditation is done by a person on the higher way, then the negative consciousness engages only in the unstained.  These two opinions, which are described in the Commentary on Difficult Points, require a fine analysis. Advice to understand two states of consciousness\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 27237,
        "course": "GK",
        "seq": 289,
        "acip": "[C7] DE YANG THEG PA GSUM GYI SLOB PA ZAG MED MNYAM GZHAG PA DANG DE GSUM GYI 'GOG PA'I SNYOMS 'JUG GI DUS SU GNAS SKABS LDOG",
        "wylie": "[c7] de yang theg pa gsum gyi slob pa zag med mnyam gzhag pa dang de gsum gyi 'gog pa'i snyoms 'jug gi dus su gnas skabs ldog",
        "english": "Temporary stopping of negative consciousness occurs with those of the three tracks who are still learning and who are in deep meditation upon the unstained; and it also occurs during periods in which those same three are in cessation meditation.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"GK\", \"acip\": \"[C7] DE YANG THEG PA GSUM GYI SLOB PA ZAG MED MNYAM GZHAG PA DANG DE GSUM GYI 'GOG PA'I SNYOMS 'JUG GI DUS SU GNAS SKABS LDOG\", \"wylie\": \"[c7] de yang theg pa gsum gyi slob pa zag med mnyam gzhag pa dang de gsum gyi 'gog pa'i snyoms 'jug gi dus su gnas skabs ldog\", \"english\": \"Temporary stopping of negative consciousness occurs with those of the three tracks who are still learning and who are in deep meditation upon the unstained; and it also occurs during periods in which those same three are in cessation meditation.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 27241,
        "course": "GK",
        "seq": 293,
        "acip": "[C11] DE YANG MKHAS PA 'GA' ZHIG GIS 'PHAGS PA GSUM GYI ZAG MED MNYAM GZHAG PA'I RGYUD LA NYON YID KYI 'KHOR NYON MONGS BZHI YANG MNGON GYUR DU 'JUG MI NUS SHING SA BON TZAM DU GNAS PA DES DE'I RGYUD KYI NYON YID KYI RDZAS RGYUN DU GYUR PA'I BDUN YID GSAL RIG ZAG BCAS SU BYED MI NUS PAS ZAG MED DU BZHED KYANG,",
        "wylie": "[c11] de yang mkhas pa 'ga' zhig gis 'phags pa gsum gyi zag med mnyam gzhag pa'i rgyud la nyon yid kyi 'khor nyon mongs bzhi yang mngon gyur du 'jug mi nus shing sa bon tsam du gnas pa des de'i rgyud kyi nyon yid kyi rdzas rgyun du gyur pa'i bdun yid gsal rig zag bcas su byed mi nus pas zag med du bzhed kyang",
        "english": "A number of masters accept the idea that—in realized beings of the three tracks who are in deep meditation on the unstained—four negative emotions accompanying negative consciousness cannot engage, and remain only in the form of mental seeds.  They say that these are unstained, since they do not have the capacity to make the raw, clear awareness here—the seventh state of mind which represents the substantial flow of negative consciousness in the person's being—something which is stained.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"GK\", \"acip\": \"[C11] DE YANG MKHAS PA 'GA' ZHIG GIS 'PHAGS PA GSUM GYI ZAG MED MNYAM GZHAG PA'I RGYUD LA NYON YID KYI 'KHOR NYON MONGS BZHI YANG MNGON GYUR DU 'JUG MI NUS SHING SA BON TZAM DU GNAS PA DES DE'I RGYUD KYI NYON YID KYI RDZAS RGYUN DU GYUR PA'I BDUN YID GSAL RIG ZAG BCAS SU BYED MI NUS PAS ZAG MED DU BZHED KYANG,\", \"wylie\": \"[c11] de yang mkhas pa 'ga' zhig gis 'phags pa gsum gyi zag med mnyam gzhag pa'i rgyud la nyon yid kyi 'khor nyon mongs bzhi yang mngon gyur du 'jug mi nus shing sa bon tsam du gnas pa des de'i rgyud kyi nyon yid kyi rdzas rgyun du gyur pa'i bdun yid gsal rig zag bcas su byed mi nus pas zag med du bzhed kyang\", \"english\": \"A number of masters accept the idea that—in realized beings of the three tracks who are in deep meditation on the unstained—four negative emotions accompanying negative consciousness cannot engage, and remain only in the form of mental seeds.  They say that these are unstained, since they do not have the capacity to make the raw, clear awareness here—the seventh state of mind which represents the substantial flow of negative consciousness in the person's being—something which is stained.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 27242,
        "course": "GK",
        "seq": 294,
        "acip": "[C12] RANG LUGS LA NYAN RANG RNAMS KYI ZAG MED MNYAM GZHAG GI DUS GANG ZAG GI BDAG MED MNGON SUM DU RTOGS PAS NYON MONGS CAN GYI ZAG BCAS SU MI 'JOG KYANG CHOS KYI BDAG 'DZIN DANG BCAS PA'I DBANG DU BYAS PA'I ZAG BCAS SU 'JOG TSUL SOGS GONG DU BSHAD PA LTAR RO,,",
        "wylie": "[c12] rang lugs la nyan rang rnams kyi zag med mnyam gzhag gi dus gang zag gi bdag med mngon sum du rtogs pas nyon mongs can gyi zag bcas su mi 'jog kyang chos kyi bdag 'dzin dang bcas pa'i dbang du byas pa'i zag bcas su 'jog tshul sogs gong du bshad pa ltar ro",
        "english": "According to our own position, listeners and self-made buddhas—when they are in deep meditation upon the unstained—are directly realizing the lack of any self-nature to the person.  As such, their state of mind cannot be established as being stained, in the sense of being involved with the negative emotions.  Nonetheless—as we have explained previously—there is for example a way of positing this state as being stained, insofar as it is possessed of the tendency to believe in the self-nature of things, as opposed to the person.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"GK\", \"acip\": \"[C12] RANG LUGS LA NYAN RANG RNAMS KYI ZAG MED MNYAM GZHAG GI DUS GANG ZAG GI BDAG MED MNGON SUM DU RTOGS PAS NYON MONGS CAN GYI ZAG BCAS SU MI 'JOG KYANG CHOS KYI BDAG 'DZIN DANG BCAS PA'I DBANG DU BYAS PA'I ZAG BCAS SU 'JOG TSUL SOGS GONG DU BSHAD PA LTAR RO,,\", \"wylie\": \"[c12] rang lugs la nyan rang rnams kyi zag med mnyam gzhag gi dus gang zag gi bdag med mngon sum du rtogs pas nyon mongs can gyi zag bcas su mi 'jog kyang chos kyi bdag 'dzin dang bcas pa'i dbang du byas pa'i zag bcas su 'jog tshul sogs gong du bshad pa ltar ro\", \"english\": \"According to our own position, listeners and self-made buddhas—when they are in deep meditation upon the unstained—are directly realizing the lack of any self-nature to the person.  As such, their state of mind cannot be established as being stained, in the sense of being involved with the negative emotions.  Nonetheless—as we have explained previously—there is for example a way of positing this state as being stained, insofar as it is possessed of the tendency to believe in the self-nature of things, as opposed to the person.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 27302,
        "course": "GK",
        "seq": 354,
        "acip": "[E34] DE DAG DGE BA'I 'OG TU MI DGE BA DANG KHAMS GONG 'OG ZAG BCAS ZAG MED SOGS SPEL NAS SKYE BAS PHAN TSUN SGO GZHI SGO BYED DU MI RUNG BA'I PHYIR DANG,",
        "wylie": "[e34] de dag dge ba'i 'og tu mi dge ba dang khams gong 'og zag bcas zag med sogs spel nas skye bas phan tshun sgo gzhi sgo byed du mi rung ba'i phyir dang",
        "english": "And their unsuitability in this regard is indeed true, because we see certain alternations with the six: first one is virtuous, and then later it becomes non-virtuous; there are variants in the higher realms, and then in the lower realms; and those which are stained, then not stained—and so on.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"GK\", \"acip\": \"[E34] DE DAG DGE BA'I 'OG TU MI DGE BA DANG KHAMS GONG 'OG ZAG BCAS ZAG MED SOGS SPEL NAS SKYE BAS PHAN TSUN SGO GZHI SGO BYED DU MI RUNG BA'I PHYIR DANG,\", \"wylie\": \"[e34] de dag dge ba'i 'og tu mi dge ba dang khams gong 'og zag bcas zag med sogs spel nas skye bas phan tshun sgo gzhi sgo byed du mi rung ba'i phyir dang\", \"english\": \"And their unsuitability in this regard is indeed true, because we see certain alternations with the six: first one is virtuous, and then later it becomes non-virtuous; there are variants in the higher realms, and then in the lower realms; and those which are stained, then not stained—and so on.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 27315,
        "course": "GK",
        "seq": 367,
        "acip": "[E47] DE BZHIN DU KUN GZHI MED NA MNYAM GZHAG ZAG MED KYANG MED PAR 'GYUR TE, DE LA GNAS PA'I GANG ZAG MED PAR 'GYUR BA'I PHYIR TE, YID SHES ZAG MED YIN PAS KHAMS GSUM GANG RUNG GI 'GRO BAR MI 'THAD CING, DE LAS GZHAN GANG ZAG GI MTSAN GZHI KHAS MA BLANGS PA'I PHYIR,",
        "wylie": "[e47] de bzhin du kun gzhi med na mnyam gzhag zag med kyang med par 'gyur te, de la gnas pa'i gang zag med par 'gyur ba'i phyir te, yid shes zag med yin pas khams gsum gang rung gi 'gro bar mi 'thad cing, de las gzhan gang zag gi mtshan gzhi khas ma blangs pa'i phyir",
        "english": "In the same manner, there can be no stainless meditation, if there is no foundation consciousness; for if there were such a thing, then there would be no person staying in the meditation. And that would be the case because—since the consciousness of the thoughts would be stainless—then it would be incorrect to say that the person possessing it could exist in any of the three different realms; and we could never accept any representative example of this particular person that was anything else.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"GK\", \"acip\": \"[E47] DE BZHIN DU KUN GZHI MED NA MNYAM GZHAG ZAG MED KYANG MED PAR 'GYUR TE, DE LA GNAS PA'I GANG ZAG MED PAR 'GYUR BA'I PHYIR TE, YID SHES ZAG MED YIN PAS KHAMS GSUM GANG RUNG GI 'GRO BAR MI 'THAD CING, DE LAS GZHAN GANG ZAG GI MTSAN GZHI KHAS MA BLANGS PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"[e47] de bzhin du kun gzhi med na mnyam gzhag zag med kyang med par 'gyur te, de la gnas pa'i gang zag med par 'gyur ba'i phyir te, yid shes zag med yin pas khams gsum gang rung gi 'gro bar mi 'thad cing, de las gzhan gang zag gi mtshan gzhi khas ma blangs pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"In the same manner, there can be no stainless meditation, if there is no foundation consciousness; for if there were such a thing, then there would be no person staying in the meditation. And that would be the case because—since the consciousness of the thoughts would be stainless—then it would be incorrect to say that the person possessing it could exist in any of the three different realms; and we could never accept any representative example of this particular person that was anything else.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 27316,
        "course": "GK",
        "seq": 368,
        "acip": "[E48] DE'I RGYUD LA GANG ZAG GI MTSAN GZHIR GYUR BA'I {%PA'I} YID KYI RNAM SHES SHIN TU PHRA BA YOD CES PAS KYANG SKYON SPONG MI NUS TE, DE 'DRA BA'I RNAM SHES DE ZAG MED SHES PA DANG RIGS MTHUN PA YIN NA SNGAR DRANGS BA'I {%PA'I} MDO DANG 'GAL BAR 'GYUR LA,",
        "wylie": "[e48] de'i rgyud la gang zag gi mtshan gzhir gyur ba'i {%pa'i} yid kyi rnam shes shin tu phra ba yod ces pas kyang skyon spong mi nus te, de 'dra ba'i rnam shes de zag med shes pa dang rigs mthun pa yin na sngar drangs ba'i {%pa'i} mdo dang 'gal bar 'gyur la",
        "english": "Now someone may reply, to our last argument, that this individual would possess within their mind the consciousness of their thoughts, which could be considered a representative example of the person. This idea though would not be enough to eliminate the problem we've raised.  For if this type of consciousness were of the same type as the unstained awareness, then that would contradict the sutra we cited earlier.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"GK\", \"acip\": \"[E48] DE'I RGYUD LA GANG ZAG GI MTSAN GZHIR GYUR BA'I {%PA'I} YID KYI RNAM SHES SHIN TU PHRA BA YOD CES PAS KYANG SKYON SPONG MI NUS TE, DE 'DRA BA'I RNAM SHES DE ZAG MED SHES PA DANG RIGS MTHUN PA YIN NA SNGAR DRANGS BA'I {%PA'I} MDO DANG 'GAL BAR 'GYUR LA,\", \"wylie\": \"[e48] de'i rgyud la gang zag gi mtshan gzhir gyur ba'i {%pa'i} yid kyi rnam shes shin tu phra ba yod ces pas kyang skyon spong mi nus te, de 'dra ba'i rnam shes de zag med shes pa dang rigs mthun pa yin na sngar drangs ba'i {%pa'i} mdo dang 'gal bar 'gyur la\", \"english\": \"Now someone may reply, to our last argument, that this individual would possess within their mind the consciousness of their thoughts, which could be considered a representative example of the person. This idea though would not be enough to eliminate the problem we've raised.  For if this type of consciousness were of the same type as the unstained awareness, then that would contradict the sutra we cited earlier.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 27576,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 257,
        "acip": "ZAG PA MED PA'I YE SHES ZHES PA'I NGO BO NI, KHA CIG MDZOD NAS BSHAD PA LTAR GYI ZAG PA RGYAS SU MI RUNG BA LA ZAG MED DU 'CHAD PA NI, LUGS 'DI'I ZAG MED DU 'JOG PA'I DON THUN MONG MA YIN PA MA RTOGS PAR SNANG BAS,",
        "wylie": "zag pa med pa'i ye shes zhes pa'i ngo bo ni, kha cig mdzod nas bshad pa ltar gyi zag pa rgyas su mi rung ba la zag med du 'chad pa ni, lugs 'di'i zag med du 'jog pa'i don thun mong ma yin pa ma rtogs par snang bas",
        "english": "What then is the essence of immaculate wisdom? Some have glossed “immaculate” here as it is explained in The Treasure House—as referring to an instance where stains can no longer spread. It would appear that these people have failed to grasp the unique way in which something is described as “immaculate” in this particular system.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"ZAG PA MED PA'I YE SHES ZHES PA'I NGO BO NI, KHA CIG MDZOD NAS BSHAD PA LTAR GYI ZAG PA RGYAS SU MI RUNG BA LA ZAG MED DU 'CHAD PA NI, LUGS 'DI'I ZAG MED DU 'JOG PA'I DON THUN MONG MA YIN PA MA RTOGS PAR SNANG BAS,\", \"wylie\": \"zag pa med pa'i ye shes zhes pa'i ngo bo ni, kha cig mdzod nas bshad pa ltar gyi zag pa rgyas su mi rung ba la zag med du 'chad pa ni, lugs 'di'i zag med du 'jog pa'i don thun mong ma yin pa ma rtogs par snang bas\", \"english\": \"What then is the essence of immaculate wisdom? Some have glossed “immaculate” here as it is explained in The Treasure House—as referring to an instance where stains can no longer spread. It would appear that these people have failed to grasp the unique way in which something is described as “immaculate” in this particular system.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 27592,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 273,
        "acip": "CHAR PHYE BA NA ZHES PA NI CHA CAN ZAG MED KYI YE SHES GCIG NYID KYI CHA SO SO SOR RIM GYIS PHYE BA'I SNGA PHYI'I CHA RNAMS SA SO SOR 'GYUR BA STE,",
        "wylie": "char phye ba na zhes pa ni cha can zag med kyi ye shes gcig nyid kyi cha so so sor rim gyis phye ba'i snga phyi'i cha rnams sa so sor 'gyur ba ste",
        "english": "The reference above to “division into certain parts” is meant to convey that there is a single whole: immaculate wisdom. We divide it into sections as it continues on, and thus arrive at progressive parts which we refer to as “bodhisattva levels.”",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"CHAR PHYE BA NA ZHES PA NI CHA CAN ZAG MED KYI YE SHES GCIG NYID KYI CHA SO SO SOR RIM GYIS PHYE BA'I SNGA PHYI'I CHA RNAMS SA SO SOR 'GYUR BA STE,\", \"wylie\": \"char phye ba na zhes pa ni cha can zag med kyi ye shes gcig nyid kyi cha so so sor rim gyis phye ba'i snga phyi'i cha rnams sa so sor 'gyur ba ste\", \"english\": \"The reference above to “division into certain parts” is meant to convey that there is a single whole: immaculate wisdom. We divide it into sections as it continues on, and thus arrive at progressive parts which we refer to as “bodhisattva levels.”\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 27953,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 634,
        "acip": "GSUM PA NI, DA NI SNGAR JI SKAD BRJOD PA'I SA RAB DGA' ZHES PA ZAG MED KYI SHES PA'I KHYAD PAR GYIS, SA DE'I YON TAN MDOR BSDUS TE BRJOD PA'I SGO NAS BSTAN PA NI,",
        "wylie": "gsum pa ni, da ni sngar ji skad brjod pa'i sa rab dga' zhes pa zag med kyi shes pa'i khyad par gyis, sa de'i yon tan mdor bsdus te brjod pa'i sgo nas bstan pa ni",
        "english": "This bring us to our third and final point from above; that is, we will give a concluding summary of the first level, named Perfect Happiness, by describing its high qualities, singling out the quality of immaculate knowledge.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"GSUM PA NI, DA NI SNGAR JI SKAD BRJOD PA'I SA RAB DGA' ZHES PA ZAG MED KYI SHES PA'I KHYAD PAR GYIS, SA DE'I YON TAN MDOR BSDUS TE BRJOD PA'I SGO NAS BSTAN PA NI,\", \"wylie\": \"gsum pa ni, da ni sngar ji skad brjod pa'i sa rab dga' zhes pa zag med kyi shes pa'i khyad par gyis, sa de'i yon tan mdor bsdus te brjod pa'i sgo nas bstan pa ni\", \"english\": \"This bring us to our third and final point from above; that is, we will give a concluding summary of the first level, named Perfect Happiness, by describing its high qualities, singling out the quality of immaculate knowledge.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 28035,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 716,
        "acip": "DE 'DRA BA'I TSUL KHRIMS 'JIG RTEN PA'I PHA ROL TU PHYIN PA ZHES BSHAD LA, TSUL KHRIMS DE NYID SNGAR BSHAD PA'I 'KHOR GSUM DU BDEN PAR DMIGS PA'I CHAGS PAS STONG PA STE MI DMIGS PAR RTOGS PA'I ZAG MED KYI SHES RAB KYIS ZIN NA, 'JIG RTEN LAS 'DAS PA'I PHA ROL TU PHYIN PA YIN PAS TSUL KHRIMS LA GNYIS KYI DBYE BA YOD DO,,",
        "wylie": "de 'dra ba'i tshul khrims 'jig rten pa'i pha rol tu phyin pa zhes bshad la, tshul khrims de nyid sngar bshad pa'i 'khor gsum du bden par dmigs pa'i chags pas stong pa ste mi dmigs par rtogs pa'i zag med kyi shes rab kyis zin na, 'jig rten las 'das pa'i pha rol tu phyin pa yin pas tshul khrims la gnyis kyi dbye ba yod do",
        "english": "This is the kind of ethical way of life that we call “a perfection of the world.” Suppose though that this same way of life is empty of attachment—in the form of seeing the three spheres we mentioned before as existing in truth; that is, suppose this lifestyle is imbued with immaculate wisdom, where we have realized that these things are non-existent. This then is a perfection which is beyond the world—and so there is a division of ethics into two. Harvest moon",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE 'DRA BA'I TSUL KHRIMS 'JIG RTEN PA'I PHA ROL TU PHYIN PA ZHES BSHAD LA, TSUL KHRIMS DE NYID SNGAR BSHAD PA'I 'KHOR GSUM DU BDEN PAR DMIGS PA'I CHAGS PAS STONG PA STE MI DMIGS PAR RTOGS PA'I ZAG MED KYI SHES RAB KYIS ZIN NA, 'JIG RTEN LAS 'DAS PA'I PHA ROL TU PHYIN PA YIN PAS TSUL KHRIMS LA GNYIS KYI DBYE BA YOD DO,,\", \"wylie\": \"de 'dra ba'i tshul khrims 'jig rten pa'i pha rol tu phyin pa zhes bshad la, tshul khrims de nyid sngar bshad pa'i 'khor gsum du bden par dmigs pa'i chags pas stong pa ste mi dmigs par rtogs pa'i zag med kyi shes rab kyis zin na, 'jig rten las 'das pa'i pha rol tu phyin pa yin pas tshul khrims la gnyis kyi dbye ba yod do\", \"english\": \"This is the kind of ethical way of life that we call “a perfection of the world.” Suppose though that this same way of life is empty of attachment—in the form of seeing the three spheres we mentioned before as existing in truth; that is, suppose this lifestyle is imbued with immaculate wisdom, where we have realized that these things are non-existent. This then is a perfection which is beyond the world—and so there is a division of ethics into two. Harvest moon\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 28162,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 843,
        "acip": "BDEN 'DZIN NYON MONGS SU 'JOG PA'I LUGS 'DI LA, NYON MONGS ZAG MED KYI LAM GYIS SPONG BA NA, BDEN 'DZIN RE'I SA BON SPONG DGOS PAS SA BON DE LAS GZHAN PA'I GNYIS SNANG 'KHRUL PA'I BAG CHAGS SHES SGRIB TU BZHAG PA'I PHYOGS RE YANG GTAN ZAD MI NUS PAS, NYON MONGS THAMS CAD MA ZAD BAR DU SHES SGRIB MI SPONG BA'I PHYIR, SHES SGRIB NI DAG PA SA GSUM GYI SKABS SU SPONG NGO,,",
        "wylie": "bden 'dzin nyon mongs su 'jog pa'i lugs 'di la, nyon mongs zag med kyi lam gyis spong ba na, bden 'dzin re'i sa bon spong dgos pas sa bon de las gzhan pa'i gnyis snang 'khrul pa'i bag chags shes sgrib tu bzhag pa'i phyogs re yang gtan zad mi nus pas, nyon mongs thams cad ma zad bar du shes sgrib mi spong ba'i phyir, shes sgrib ni dag pa sa gsum gyi skabs su spong ngo",
        "english": "Here in this system, where holding that things are real is classified as a negative emotion, each seed of holding things this way must be eliminated where negativities are eliminated by the path of the immaculate. But not all the different propensities for seeing things in a mistaken way—with a discrepancy—which are other than these seeds, and which we classify as obstacles to omniscience, can at that point be eliminated permanently. As such, we cannot rid ourselves of the obstacles to omniscience until we finish off all the negative emotions; and therefore it is during the three pure levels that we rid ourselves of these omniscience obstacles.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"BDEN 'DZIN NYON MONGS SU 'JOG PA'I LUGS 'DI LA, NYON MONGS ZAG MED KYI LAM GYIS SPONG BA NA, BDEN 'DZIN RE'I SA BON SPONG DGOS PAS SA BON DE LAS GZHAN PA'I GNYIS SNANG 'KHRUL PA'I BAG CHAGS SHES SGRIB TU BZHAG PA'I PHYOGS RE YANG GTAN ZAD MI NUS PAS, NYON MONGS THAMS CAD MA ZAD BAR DU SHES SGRIB MI SPONG BA'I PHYIR, SHES SGRIB NI DAG PA SA GSUM GYI SKABS SU SPONG NGO,,\", \"wylie\": \"bden 'dzin nyon mongs su 'jog pa'i lugs 'di la, nyon mongs zag med kyi lam gyis spong ba na, bden 'dzin re'i sa bon spong dgos pas sa bon de las gzhan pa'i gnyis snang 'khrul pa'i bag chags shes sgrib tu bzhag pa'i phyogs re yang gtan zad mi nus pas, nyon mongs thams cad ma zad bar du shes sgrib mi spong ba'i phyir, shes sgrib ni dag pa sa gsum gyi skabs su spong ngo\", \"english\": \"Here in this system, where holding that things are real is classified as a negative emotion, each seed of holding things this way must be eliminated where negativities are eliminated by the path of the immaculate. But not all the different propensities for seeing things in a mistaken way—with a discrepancy—which are other than these seeds, and which we classify as obstacles to omniscience, can at that point be eliminated permanently. As such, we cannot rid ourselves of the obstacles to omniscience until we finish off all the negative emotions; and therefore it is during the three pure levels that we rid ourselves of these omniscience obstacles.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 28217,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 898,
        "acip": "RANG LUGS LA 'GOG PA MNGON SUM DU SHES PA MNYAM GZHAG ZAG MED KYI YE SHES KYIS, ,DE KHO NA NYID KYI DON RTOGS PA'I STENG NAS BSGRUBS PAS, 'GOG BDEN KUN RDZOB KYI BDEN PA YIN NA RNAM GZHAG DE DAG GTAN MI RUNG ZHING,",
        "wylie": "rang lugs la 'gog pa mngon sum du shes pa mnyam gzhag zag med kyi ye shes kyis, ,de kho na nyid kyi don rtogs pa'i steng nas bsgrubs pas, 'gog bden kun rdzob kyi bden pa yin na rnam gzhag de dag gtan mi rung zhing",
        "english": "According to our own school, immaculate wisdom engaged in deep meditation, a direct perception of the end of suffering, sees both the object of suchness and—on top of that—this end. As such, saying that the truth of the end of suffering is part of deceptive reality would—according to this presentation—be completely mistaken.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"RANG LUGS LA 'GOG PA MNGON SUM DU SHES PA MNYAM GZHAG ZAG MED KYI YE SHES KYIS, ,DE KHO NA NYID KYI DON RTOGS PA'I STENG NAS BSGRUBS PAS, 'GOG BDEN KUN RDZOB KYI BDEN PA YIN NA RNAM GZHAG DE DAG GTAN MI RUNG ZHING,\", \"wylie\": \"rang lugs la 'gog pa mngon sum du shes pa mnyam gzhag zag med kyi ye shes kyis, ,de kho na nyid kyi don rtogs pa'i steng nas bsgrubs pas, 'gog bden kun rdzob kyi bden pa yin na rnam gzhag de dag gtan mi rung zhing\", \"english\": \"According to our own school, immaculate wisdom engaged in deep meditation, a direct perception of the end of suffering, sees both the object of suchness and—on top of that—this end. As such, saying that the truth of the end of suffering is part of deceptive reality would—according to this presentation—be completely mistaken.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 29247,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 1928,
        "acip": "DE'I TSE YID KYI SHES PA NI GNYIS SNANG 'KHRUL BA'I BAG CHAGS KYIS MA BSLAD PA'I ZAG MED YIN PAS, DE'I NGO BOR BAG LA NYAL DE GNAS PA MI RIGS LA, DBANG SHES KYANG DE'I RTEN DU MED CING GZUGS KYANG DE'I RTEN DU MI RIGS LA",
        "wylie": "de'i tshe yid kyi shes pa ni gnyis snang 'khrul ba'i bag chags kyis ma bslad pa'i zag med yin pas, de'i ngo bor bag la nyal de gnas pa mi rigs la, dbang shes kyang de'i rten du med cing gzugs kyang de'i rten du mi rigs la",
        "english": "At this point though, the awareness of our thought is unstained, in the sense that it is not infected by the deep seeds for the error of the discrepancy. As such, it would be incorrect to say that the seeds were remaining in a dormant state as part & parcel with this awareness. Neither could there be any sense consciousness which were acting as their basis; and it would be illogical to say that physical form itself were providing the base.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE'I TSE YID KYI SHES PA NI GNYIS SNANG 'KHRUL BA'I BAG CHAGS KYIS MA BSLAD PA'I ZAG MED YIN PAS, DE'I NGO BOR BAG LA NYAL DE GNAS PA MI RIGS LA, DBANG SHES KYANG DE'I RTEN DU MED CING GZUGS KYANG DE'I RTEN DU MI RIGS LA\", \"wylie\": \"de'i tshe yid kyi shes pa ni gnyis snang 'khrul ba'i bag chags kyis ma bslad pa'i zag med yin pas, de'i ngo bor bag la nyal de gnas pa mi rigs la, dbang shes kyang de'i rten du med cing gzugs kyang de'i rten du mi rigs la\", \"english\": \"At this point though, the awareness of our thought is unstained, in the sense that it is not infected by the deep seeds for the error of the discrepancy. As such, it would be incorrect to say that the seeds were remaining in a dormant state as part & parcel with this awareness. Neither could there be any sense consciousness which were acting as their basis; and it would be illogical to say that physical form itself were providing the base.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 29250,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 1931,
        "acip": "LUGS 'DI'I GANG ZAG GI 'JOG TSUL THUN MONG MA YIN PA SHES NA NAM MKHA' DANG RNAM SHES MTHA' YAS DANG CI YANG MED PA GSUM DU SKYES PA'I 'PHAGS PA'I RGYUD LA, 'JIG RTEN LAS 'DAS PA ZAG MED KYI SEMS MNGON DU GYUR PA NA, 'JIG RTEN PA'I SEMS GZHAN MED PAS DE RNAMS KYI 'GRO BA LDOG PAR 'GYUR RO, ZHES PA DANG,",
        "wylie": "lugs 'di'i gang zag gi 'jog tshul thun mong ma yin pa shes na nam mkha' dang rnam shes mtha' yas dang ci yang med pa gsum du skyes pa'i 'phags pa'i rgyud la, 'jig rten las 'das pa zag med kyi sems mngon du gyur pa na, 'jig rten pa'i sems gzhan med pas de rnams kyi 'gro ba ldog par 'gyur ro, zhes pa dang",
        "english": "If we can come to an understanding of the unique manner in which this particular system posits the person, then it would appear that our position could no longer be challenged by arguments such as the following. The first here would be where someone asks us to consider the mind stream of a realized being who has taken birth into one of the three levels of Limitless Space; Limitless Awareness; or Nothing At All. When a state of mind which transcends the normal world—an unstained state of mind—manifests itself, then since there can be no other, worldly states of mind, their functioning is said to be suspended.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"LUGS 'DI'I GANG ZAG GI 'JOG TSUL THUN MONG MA YIN PA SHES NA NAM MKHA' DANG RNAM SHES MTHA' YAS DANG CI YANG MED PA GSUM DU SKYES PA'I 'PHAGS PA'I RGYUD LA, 'JIG RTEN LAS 'DAS PA ZAG MED KYI SEMS MNGON DU GYUR PA NA, 'JIG RTEN PA'I SEMS GZHAN MED PAS DE RNAMS KYI 'GRO BA LDOG PAR 'GYUR RO, ZHES PA DANG,\", \"wylie\": \"lugs 'di'i gang zag gi 'jog tshul thun mong ma yin pa shes na nam mkha' dang rnam shes mtha' yas dang ci yang med pa gsum du skyes pa'i 'phags pa'i rgyud la, 'jig rten las 'das pa zag med kyi sems mngon du gyur pa na, 'jig rten pa'i sems gzhan med pas de rnams kyi 'gro ba ldog par 'gyur ro, zhes pa dang\", \"english\": \"If we can come to an understanding of the unique manner in which this particular system posits the person, then it would appear that our position could no longer be challenged by arguments such as the following. The first here would be where someone asks us to consider the mind stream of a realized being who has taken birth into one of the three levels of Limitless Space; Limitless Awareness; or Nothing At All. When a state of mind which transcends the normal world—an unstained state of mind—manifests itself, then since there can be no other, worldly states of mind, their functioning is said to be suspended.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 29251,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 1932,
        "acip": "SRID RTZER SKYES PA'I 'PHAGS PA LA CI YANG MED PA'I SAR GTOGS PA'I ZAG MED KYI SEMS MNGON DU GYUR PA NA, SRID RTZE DANG CI YANG MED PA GNYIS KA'I SAS BSDUS PA'I 'GRO BA LDOG PAR 'GYUR TE, ZAG MED KYI SEMS DE'I GNAS NI 'GRO BA DE GNYIS DANG, MYANG NGAN LAS 'DAS PA'I 'GRO BAR MI RUNG BA'I PHYIR RO, ZHES GSUNGS PA'I",
        "wylie": "srid rtser skyes pa'i 'phags pa la ci yang med pa'i sar gtogs pa'i zag med kyi sems mngon du gyur pa na, srid rtse dang ci yang med pa gnyis ka'i sas bsdus pa'i 'gro ba ldog par 'gyur te, zag med kyi sems de'i gnas ni 'gro ba de gnyis dang, myang ngan las 'das pa'i 'gro bar mi rung ba'i phyir ro, zhes gsungs pa'i",
        "english": "Or else we can consider the case where a realized being has taken birth into the level known as Peak of Existence, but they have manifested an unstained state of mind taken in by the level of Nothing At All. This then blocks any possibility of them passing to a state subsumed by either the Peak of Existence or by Nothing At All, since it would be inappropriate to describe the location of an unstained state of mind as either one of these two types of existence, or as a state of existence within the state of nirvana.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"SRID RTZER SKYES PA'I 'PHAGS PA LA CI YANG MED PA'I SAR GTOGS PA'I ZAG MED KYI SEMS MNGON DU GYUR PA NA, SRID RTZE DANG CI YANG MED PA GNYIS KA'I SAS BSDUS PA'I 'GRO BA LDOG PAR 'GYUR TE, ZAG MED KYI SEMS DE'I GNAS NI 'GRO BA DE GNYIS DANG, MYANG NGAN LAS 'DAS PA'I 'GRO BAR MI RUNG BA'I PHYIR RO, ZHES GSUNGS PA'I\", \"wylie\": \"srid rtser skyes pa'i 'phags pa la ci yang med pa'i sar gtogs pa'i zag med kyi sems mngon du gyur pa na, srid rtse dang ci yang med pa gnyis ka'i sas bsdus pa'i 'gro ba ldog par 'gyur te, zag med kyi sems de'i gnas ni 'gro ba de gnyis dang, myang ngan las 'das pa'i 'gro bar mi rung ba'i phyir ro, zhes gsungs pa'i\", \"english\": \"Or else we can consider the case where a realized being has taken birth into the level known as Peak of Existence, but they have manifested an unstained state of mind taken in by the level of Nothing At All. This then blocks any possibility of them passing to a state subsumed by either the Peak of Existence or by Nothing At All, since it would be inappropriate to describe the location of an unstained state of mind as either one of these two types of existence, or as a state of existence within the state of nirvana.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 29252,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 1933,
        "acip": "RIGS PA RNAMS KYIS KYANG MI GNOD PAR MNGON TE, ZAG BCAS DANG ZAG MED KYI SEMS GANG YANG 'GRO BA DE DAG GI MTSAN GZHIR MA BZHAG KYANG 'GRO BA BZHAG PAS CHOG PA'I PHYIR RO,,",
        "wylie": "rigs pa rnams kyis kyang mi gnod par mngon te, zag bcas dang zag med kyi sems gang yang 'gro ba de dag gi mtshan gzhir ma bzhag kyang 'gro ba bzhag pas chog pa'i phyir ro",
        "english": "The reason that these arguments would no longer be a challenge is that—even though there is no state of mind either stained or unstained which we could point to as a typical example for any of these states of existence—we can nonetheless posit such states.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"RIGS PA RNAMS KYIS KYANG MI GNOD PAR MNGON TE, ZAG BCAS DANG ZAG MED KYI SEMS GANG YANG 'GRO BA DE DAG GI MTSAN GZHIR MA BZHAG KYANG 'GRO BA BZHAG PAS CHOG PA'I PHYIR RO,,\", \"wylie\": \"rigs pa rnams kyis kyang mi gnod par mngon te, zag bcas dang zag med kyi sems gang yang 'gro ba de dag gi mtshan gzhir ma bzhag kyang 'gro ba bzhag pas chog pa'i phyir ro\", \"english\": \"The reason that these arguments would no longer be a challenge is that—even though there is no state of mind either stained or unstained which we could point to as a typical example for any of these states of existence—we can nonetheless posit such states.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 31637,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 4318,
        "acip": "DE GNYIS LAS BZLOG PA STE LAS 'DRES MA DANG, ZAG BCAS KYI LAS NGAN PA ZAD PAR BYED PA'I ZAG MED KYI DNGOS PO'I LAS RNAMS DANG, LAM DE'I RNAM SMIN SHIN TU MI 'DRA BA SNA TSOGS PA LA'ANG,",
        "wylie": "de gnyis las bzlog pa ste las 'dres ma dang, zag bcas kyi las ngan pa zad par byed pa'i zag med kyi dngos po'i las rnams dang, lam de'i rnam smin shin tu mi 'dra ba sna tshogs pa la'ang",
        "english": "And then there are cases of the opposite of these two; meaning that the karmas are mixed. And then there are the karmas that represent entities which are immaculate—which in fact function to finish off negative karmas. And then there are the great variety of different ripenings that are produced by this path.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE GNYIS LAS BZLOG PA STE LAS 'DRES MA DANG, ZAG BCAS KYI LAS NGAN PA ZAD PAR BYED PA'I ZAG MED KYI DNGOS PO'I LAS RNAMS DANG, LAM DE'I RNAM SMIN SHIN TU MI 'DRA BA SNA TSOGS PA LA'ANG,\", \"wylie\": \"de gnyis las bzlog pa ste las 'dres ma dang, zag bcas kyi las ngan pa zad par byed pa'i zag med kyi dngos po'i las rnams dang, lam de'i rnam smin shin tu mi 'dra ba sna tshogs pa la'ang\", \"english\": \"And then there are cases of the opposite of these two; meaning that the karmas are mixed. And then there are the karmas that represent entities which are immaculate—which in fact function to finish off negative karmas. And then there are the great variety of different ripenings that are produced by this path.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 31670,
        "course": "ILL",
        "seq": 4351,
        "acip": "SLOB MA NYAN THOS DANG LA SOGS PAS BSDUS PA RANG RKYAL {%RGYAL} BA RNAMS KYIS NYON MONGS PA RNAMS BLO STE ZAG MED KYI SHES RAB KYIS 'GOG PA STE SPANGS PA",
        "wylie": "slob ma nyan thos dang la sogs pas bsdus pa rang rkyal {%rgyal} ba rnams kyis nyon mongs pa rnams blo ste zag med kyi shes rab kyis 'gog pa ste spangs pa",
        "english": "Those who are students, or listeners, and “the rest”—which is a reference to the self-made buddhas—put a stop to, or eliminate, everything involved with the negative emotions by using their intelligence; here referring to wisdom.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"SLOB MA NYAN THOS DANG LA SOGS PAS BSDUS PA RANG RKYAL {%RGYAL} BA RNAMS KYIS NYON MONGS PA RNAMS BLO STE ZAG MED KYI SHES RAB KYIS 'GOG PA STE SPANGS PA\", \"wylie\": \"slob ma nyan thos dang la sogs pas bsdus pa rang rkyal {%rgyal} ba rnams kyis nyon mongs pa rnams blo ste zag med kyi shes rab kyis 'gog pa ste spangs pa\", \"english\": \"Those who are students, or listeners, and “the rest”—which is a reference to the self-made buddhas—put a stop to, or eliminate, everything involved with the negative emotions by using their intelligence; here referring to wisdom.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 34461,
        "course": "P3",
        "seq": 48,
        "acip": "BYANG TSE GTONG LEN MDOR BSDUS BSGOM TSUL NI DE GNYIS RLUNG LA BSKYON PAR BYA ZHES, GSUNGS DE YANG RANG GI SNA BUG GNYIS NAS RLUNG PHYIR LA 'GRO TSE LUS DANG LONGS SPYOD DANG DGE BA SEMS CAN KUN LA BTANG BA YIS DE DAG ZAG MED BDE BA MTHAR THUG DANG LDAN PAR BSAM ZHING NANG DU RNGUB PA'I TSE SEMS CAN KUN GYI SDUG BSNGAL RGYU BCAS RNAMS RANG GI SNYING GI DKYIL DU BLANGS PA YIS DE DAG SDUG BSNGAL KUN DANG BRAL BAR BSAM DE LTAR GOM NA RLUNG SEMS GNYIS PO NI 'JUG PA GCIG PAS GZHAN DU MI YENGS SHING DRAN SHES LDAN PAS STOBS NI CHE BAR GSUNGS",
        "wylie": "byang tshe gtong len mdor bsdus bsgom tshul ni de gnyis rlung la bskyon par bya zhes, gsungs de yang rang gi sna bug gnyis nas rlung phyir la 'gro tshe lus dang longs spyod dang dge ba sems can kun la btang ba yis de dag zag med bde ba mthar thug dang ldan par bsam zhing nang du rngub pa'i tshe sems can kun gyi sdug bsngal rgyu bcas rnams rang gi snying gi dkyil du blangs pa yis de dag sdug bsngal kun dang bral bar bsam de ltar gom na rlung sems gnyis po ni 'jug pa gcig pas gzhan du mi yengs shing dran shes ldan pas stobs ni che bar gsungs",
        "english": "When you want to do a meditation to master these points, here is a brief description of how to proceed, through the practice of giving and taking: Remember the text that says to \"let these two ride on the wind\"; For this you take the breath that passes out of both your nostrils And give with it to every living being first your body, And then all your possessions, followed by your virtuous acts. Imagine then that they have found the ultimate happiness, Free of all impurity. Now when you inhale your breath See it coming with the pain and cause of pain for every Living being, deep into the center of your heart; Imagine then that they are free of every pain there is. It's said that doing the practice in this way is especially powerful, For the reason that—because the mind and winds both move in tandem— You'll be able to avoid distraction, and keep tight recollection. Here next are verses from the Abbreviation of the Essence, a text on mental training, again by the first Changkya Rinpoche: Contemplation Eight",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"P3\", \"acip\": \"BYANG TSE GTONG LEN MDOR BSDUS BSGOM TSUL NI DE GNYIS RLUNG LA BSKYON PAR BYA ZHES, GSUNGS DE YANG RANG GI SNA BUG GNYIS NAS RLUNG PHYIR LA 'GRO TSE LUS DANG LONGS SPYOD DANG DGE BA SEMS CAN KUN LA BTANG BA YIS DE DAG ZAG MED BDE BA MTHAR THUG DANG LDAN PAR BSAM ZHING NANG DU RNGUB PA'I TSE SEMS CAN KUN GYI SDUG BSNGAL RGYU BCAS RNAMS RANG GI SNYING GI DKYIL DU BLANGS PA YIS DE DAG SDUG BSNGAL KUN DANG BRAL BAR BSAM DE LTAR GOM NA RLUNG SEMS GNYIS PO NI 'JUG PA GCIG PAS GZHAN DU MI YENGS SHING DRAN SHES LDAN PAS STOBS NI CHE BAR GSUNGS\", \"wylie\": \"byang tshe gtong len mdor bsdus bsgom tshul ni de gnyis rlung la bskyon par bya zhes, gsungs de yang rang gi sna bug gnyis nas rlung phyir la 'gro tshe lus dang longs spyod dang dge ba sems can kun la btang ba yis de dag zag med bde ba mthar thug dang ldan par bsam zhing nang du rngub pa'i tshe sems can kun gyi sdug bsngal rgyu bcas rnams rang gi snying gi dkyil du blangs pa yis de dag sdug bsngal kun dang bral bar bsam de ltar gom na rlung sems gnyis po ni 'jug pa gcig pas gzhan du mi yengs shing dran shes ldan pas stobs ni che bar gsungs\", \"english\": \"When you want to do a meditation to master these points, here is a brief description of how to proceed, through the practice of giving and taking: Remember the text that says to \\\"let these two ride on the wind\\\"; For this you take the breath that passes out of both your nostrils And give with it to every living being first your body, And then all your possessions, followed by your virtuous acts. Imagine then that they have found the ultimate happiness, Free of all impurity. Now when you inhale your breath See it coming with the pain and cause of pain for every Living being, deep into the center of your heart; Imagine then that they are free of every pain there is. It's said that doing the practice in this way is especially powerful, For the reason that—because the mind and winds both move in tandem— You'll be able to avoid distraction, and keep tight recollection. Here next are verses from the Abbreviation of the Essence, a text on mental training, again by the first Changkya Rinpoche: Contemplation Eight\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 0.971}"
      },
      {
        "id": 34486,
        "course": "P3",
        "seq": 73,
        "acip": "PHAR GTONG BA'I TSE SKYABS 'GRO DANG LAS 'BRAS KYI DGE RTZA DANG ZAG MED KYI BDE BA RANG LA, MED KYANG YOD PAR MOS TE NGAN SONG GSUM LA BTANG BAS BDE 'GRO THOB LAM BSLAB PA GSUM GYI DGE RTZA MNGON MTHO'I GNAS LA BTANG BAS THAR PA THOB BYANG CHUB KYI SEMS DANG RGYAL SRAS KYI SPYOD PA'I DGE RTZA RNAMS NYAN RANG SOGS LA BTANG BAS SANGS RGYAS THOB PAR BSAM",
        "wylie": "phar gtong ba'i tshe skyabs 'gro dang las 'bras kyi dge rtsa dang zag med kyi bde ba rang la, med kyang yod par mos te ngan song gsum la btang bas bde 'gro thob lam bslab pa gsum gyi dge rtsa mngon mtho'i gnas la btang bas thar pa thob byang chub kyi sems dang rgyal sras kyi spyod pa'i dge rtsa rnams nyan rang sogs la btang bas sangs rgyas thob par bsam",
        "english": "When you do the practice of giving as you breathe out, think of the quantity of virtue you have in you from your acts of taking refuge, and observing the laws of karma and its consequences. Also imagine that you have some immaculate happiness to give, even though at this point we have yet to gain any. Do the act of giving all these, focusing on the those in the lower realms. See them reaching the higher realms as a result. Next take the quantity of virtue that you possess from following the path of the three trainings. Give it out to those in the higher realms, and imagine that Take finally the quantity of virtue you have from learning the Wish for enlightenment, and from following the way of life of the noble sons and daughters of the Buddhas. Imagine that you have given this virtue away to people like the listeners and the self-made buddhas. Imagine finally that this helps them reach Buddhahood itself.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"P3\", \"acip\": \"PHAR GTONG BA'I TSE SKYABS 'GRO DANG LAS 'BRAS KYI DGE RTZA DANG ZAG MED KYI BDE BA RANG LA, MED KYANG YOD PAR MOS TE NGAN SONG GSUM LA BTANG BAS BDE 'GRO THOB LAM BSLAB PA GSUM GYI DGE RTZA MNGON MTHO'I GNAS LA BTANG BAS THAR PA THOB BYANG CHUB KYI SEMS DANG RGYAL SRAS KYI SPYOD PA'I DGE RTZA RNAMS NYAN RANG SOGS LA BTANG BAS SANGS RGYAS THOB PAR BSAM\", \"wylie\": \"phar gtong ba'i tshe skyabs 'gro dang las 'bras kyi dge rtsa dang zag med kyi bde ba rang la, med kyang yod par mos te ngan song gsum la btang bas bde 'gro thob lam bslab pa gsum gyi dge rtsa mngon mtho'i gnas la btang bas thar pa thob byang chub kyi sems dang rgyal sras kyi spyod pa'i dge rtsa rnams nyan rang sogs la btang bas sangs rgyas thob par bsam\", \"english\": \"When you do the practice of giving as you breathe out, think of the quantity of virtue you have in you from your acts of taking refuge, and observing the laws of karma and its consequences. Also imagine that you have some immaculate happiness to give, even though at this point we have yet to gain any. Do the act of giving all these, focusing on the those in the lower realms. See them reaching the higher realms as a result. Next take the quantity of virtue that you possess from following the path of the three trainings. Give it out to those in the higher realms, and imagine that Take finally the quantity of virtue you have from learning the Wish for enlightenment, and from following the way of life of the noble sons and daughters of the Buddhas. Imagine that you have given this virtue away to people like the listeners and the self-made buddhas. Imagine finally that this helps them reach Buddhahood itself.\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 1.0}"
      },
      {
        "id": 34487,
        "course": "P3",
        "seq": 74,
        "acip": "MDOR NA SNOD BCUD THAMS CAD DAG PA RAB 'BYAMS 'BA' ZHIG TU BSAM LA RANG GI LUS, LONGS SPYOD DGE RTZA THAMS CAD KYANG MCHOD SPRIN MTHA' YAS PA'I RNAM PAR SPRUL TE PHUL BAS RGYAL BA RNAMS KYI THUGS RGYUD LA ZAG MED KYI BDE STONG GSAR DU 'KHRUNGS PA'I MOS PA BYAS",
        "wylie": "mdor na snod bcud thams cad dag pa rab 'byams 'ba' zhig tu bsam la rang gi lus, longs spyod dge rtsa thams cad kyang mchod sprin mtha' yas pa'i rnam par sprul te phul bas rgyal ba rnams kyi thugs rgyud la zag med kyi bde stong gsar du 'khrungs pa'i mos pa byas",
        "english": "Contemplation Eleven",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"P3\", \"acip\": \"MDOR NA SNOD BCUD THAMS CAD DAG PA RAB 'BYAMS 'BA' ZHIG TU BSAM LA RANG GI LUS, LONGS SPYOD DGE RTZA THAMS CAD KYANG MCHOD SPRIN MTHA' YAS PA'I RNAM PAR SPRUL TE PHUL BAS RGYAL BA RNAMS KYI THUGS RGYUD LA ZAG MED KYI BDE STONG GSAR DU 'KHRUNGS PA'I MOS PA BYAS\", \"wylie\": \"mdor na snod bcud thams cad dag pa rab 'byams 'ba' zhig tu bsam la rang gi lus, longs spyod dge rtsa thams cad kyang mchod sprin mtha' yas pa'i rnam par sprul te phul bas rgyal ba rnams kyi thugs rgyud la zag med kyi bde stong gsar du 'khrungs pa'i mos pa byas\", \"english\": \"Contemplation Eleven\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 1.0}"
      },
      {
        "id": 34895,
        "course": "P7",
        "seq": 6,
        "acip": "'O NA MTHONG LAM BAR CHAD MED LAM GYI DUS SU DE'I SPANG BYA MTHONG SPANG MED MOD KYANG, SGOM SPANG GI BAG LA NYAL YOD DGOS LA DE'I TSE YID KYI SHES PA NI GNYIS SNANG 'KHRUL PA'I BAG CHAGS KYIS MA BSLAD PA'I ZAG MED YIN PAS DE'I NGO BOR BAG LA NYAL DE GNAS PA MI RIGS LA DBANG SHES KYANG DE'I RTEN DU MED CING GZUGS KYANG DE'I RTEN DU MI RIGS LA KUN GZHI YANG MI 'DOD PAS DE'I RTEN MED PAR 'GYUR RO SNYAM NA SKYON MED DE DE'I TSE NGA TZAM ZHIG SGOM SPANG GI BAG LA NYAL GYI RTEN YIN PA'I PHYIR TE SPANG GNYEN GZHAN LA YANG DE BZHIN DU SHES PAR BYA'O",
        "wylie": "'o na mthong lam bar chad med lam gyi dus su de'i spang bya mthong spang med mod kyang, sgom spang gi bag la nyal yod dgos la de'i tshe yid kyi shes pa ni gnyis snang 'khrul pa'i bag chags kyis ma bslad pa'i zag med yin pas de'i ngo bor bag la nyal de gnas pa mi rigs la dbang shes kyang de'i rten du med cing gzugs kyang de'i rten du mi rigs la kun gzhi yang mi 'dod pas de'i rten med par 'gyur ro snyam na skyon med de de'i tshe nga tsam zhig sgom spang gi bag la nyal gyi rten yin pa'i phyir te spang gnyen gzhan la yang de bzhin du shes par bya'o",
        "english": "One might wonder about something else: Let's talk about the period while you are in the \"uninterrupted\" stage of the path of seeing [the actual direct perception of emptiness]. I can accept that at this point the negative thoughts eliminated by this path are no longer present, but we would have to say that the negative things eliminated by the path of habituation are still there, in a dormant way. At this particular point in time, the consciousness of the mind is unstained, unaffected by the mental seed which causes the mistaken state of mind where the appearance and actuality of things are different from one another. As such none of these things could lie dormant here, due to its very quality at the time. There is no consciousness of the senses that could act as the basis for those mental seeds, and it would be improper to say that physical form could ever provide such a basis either. According to you, moreover, there's no foundation consciousness. I would have to think then that there is no basis at all where these dormant things And yet there is no such problem, for at this point in time it is the simple \"me\" which is providing the basis for the dormant things eliminated by the path of habituation to stay. You can apply this reasoning as well to all the other cases involving things to be eliminated, and the antidotes which eliminate them. Selection Three: The Middle-Way School on the question of where the seeds of karma stay until they give their result",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"P7\", \"acip\": \"'O NA MTHONG LAM BAR CHAD MED LAM GYI DUS SU DE'I SPANG BYA MTHONG SPANG MED MOD KYANG, SGOM SPANG GI BAG LA NYAL YOD DGOS LA DE'I TSE YID KYI SHES PA NI GNYIS SNANG 'KHRUL PA'I BAG CHAGS KYIS MA BSLAD PA'I ZAG MED YIN PAS DE'I NGO BOR BAG LA NYAL DE GNAS PA MI RIGS LA DBANG SHES KYANG DE'I RTEN DU MED CING GZUGS KYANG DE'I RTEN DU MI RIGS LA KUN GZHI YANG MI 'DOD PAS DE'I RTEN MED PAR 'GYUR RO SNYAM NA SKYON MED DE DE'I TSE NGA TZAM ZHIG SGOM SPANG GI BAG LA NYAL GYI RTEN YIN PA'I PHYIR TE SPANG GNYEN GZHAN LA YANG DE BZHIN DU SHES PAR BYA'O\", \"wylie\": \"'o na mthong lam bar chad med lam gyi dus su de'i spang bya mthong spang med mod kyang, sgom spang gi bag la nyal yod dgos la de'i tshe yid kyi shes pa ni gnyis snang 'khrul pa'i bag chags kyis ma bslad pa'i zag med yin pas de'i ngo bor bag la nyal de gnas pa mi rigs la dbang shes kyang de'i rten du med cing gzugs kyang de'i rten du mi rigs la kun gzhi yang mi 'dod pas de'i rten med par 'gyur ro snyam na skyon med de de'i tshe nga tsam zhig sgom spang gi bag la nyal gyi rten yin pa'i phyir te spang gnyen gzhan la yang de bzhin du shes par bya'o\", \"english\": \"One might wonder about something else: Let's talk about the period while you are in the \\\"uninterrupted\\\" stage of the path of seeing [the actual direct perception of emptiness]. I can accept that at this point the negative thoughts eliminated by this path are no longer present, but we would have to say that the negative things eliminated by the path of habituation are still there, in a dormant way. At this particular point in time, the consciousness of the mind is unstained, unaffected by the mental seed which causes the mistaken state of mind where the appearance and actuality of things are different from one another. As such none of these things could lie dormant here, due to its very quality at the time. There is no consciousness of the senses that could act as the basis for those mental seeds, and it would be improper to say that physical form could ever provide such a basis either. According to you, moreover, there's no foundation consciousness. I would have to think then that there is no basis at all where these dormant things And yet there is no such problem, for at this point in time it is the simple \\\"me\\\" which is providing the basis for the dormant things eliminated by the path of habituation to stay. You can apply this reasoning as well to all the other cases involving things to be eliminated, and the antidotes which eliminate them. Selection Three: The Middle-Way School on the question of where the seeds of karma stay until they give their result\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 1.0}"
      },
      {
        "id": 32515,
        "course": "SPF",
        "seq": 289,
        "acip": "BCOM LDAN 'DAS KYIS RAB 'BYOR LA DE MED PA'I RGYU MTSAN DE BAS NA DE BZHIN GSHEGS PA KUN BYANG GI CHOS THAMS CAD GANG ZAG GI BDAG TU GRUB PA'I SEMS CAN MED PA, SROG MED PA, GANG ZAG MED PA'O ZHES GSUNGS SO,,",
        "wylie": "bcom ldan 'das kyis rab 'byor la de med pa'i rgyu mtshan de bas na de bzhin gshegs pa kun byang gi chos thams cad gang zag gi bdag tu grub pa'i sems can med pa, srog med pa, gang zag med pa'o zhes gsungs so",
        "english": "And the Conqueror then says to Subhuti, “This is why—it is because no such thing could ever be—the One Thus Gone says that all existing things, whether they be part of the negative side of things or the enlightened, are such that no living being exists, and nothing that lives exists, and no person exists, who possesses some 'self' of the person.”",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"SPF\", \"acip\": \"BCOM LDAN 'DAS KYIS RAB 'BYOR LA DE MED PA'I RGYU MTSAN DE BAS NA DE BZHIN GSHEGS PA KUN BYANG GI CHOS THAMS CAD GANG ZAG GI BDAG TU GRUB PA'I SEMS CAN MED PA, SROG MED PA, GANG ZAG MED PA'O ZHES GSUNGS SO,,\", \"wylie\": \"bcom ldan 'das kyis rab 'byor la de med pa'i rgyu mtshan de bas na de bzhin gshegs pa kun byang gi chos thams cad gang zag gi bdag tu grub pa'i sems can med pa, srog med pa, gang zag med pa'o zhes gsungs so\", \"english\": \"And the Conqueror then says to Subhuti, “This is why—it is because no such thing could ever be—the One Thus Gone says that all existing things, whether they be part of the negative side of things or the enlightened, are such that no living being exists, and nothing that lives exists, and no person exists, who possesses some 'self' of the person.”\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 37270,
        "course": "SVN",
        "seq": 810,
        "acip": "'DOD NA, MYANG 'DAS MNGON DU BYAS PA MI 'THAD PAR THAL, PHUNG PO ZHIG PA'I DUS DER MNGON DU BYED PA PO'I GANG ZAG MED, GANG ZAG YOD DUS PHUNG PO ZHIG PA MI 'THAD PA'I PHYIR,",
        "wylie": "'dod na, myang 'das mngon du byas pa mi 'thad par thal, phung po zhig pa'i dus der mngon du byed pa po'i gang zag med, gang zag yod dus phung po zhig pa mi 'thad pa'i phyir",
        "english": "And suppose you agree to all that. But wouldn't it then be incorrect to say that someone had brought about their nirvana? Because-at that point when the parts to the person had been destroyed-wouldn't there be no person left; no one left to be the one who had brought about their nirvana? And wouldn't it be incorrect to talk about the parts to a person having been destroyed, at a point in time when the person was still present? How could we have a knowledge of the end?",
        "raw": "{\"acip\": \"'DOD NA, MYANG 'DAS MNGON DU BYAS PA MI 'THAD PAR THAL, PHUNG PO ZHIG PA'I DUS DER MNGON DU BYED PA PO'I GANG ZAG MED, GANG ZAG YOD DUS PHUNG PO ZHIG PA MI 'THAD PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"'dod na, myang 'das mngon du byas pa mi 'thad par thal, phung po zhig pa'i dus der mngon du byed pa po'i gang zag med, gang zag yod dus phung po zhig pa mi 'thad pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"And suppose you agree to all that. But wouldn't it then be incorrect to say that someone had brought about their nirvana? Because-at that point when the parts to the person had been destroyed-wouldn't there be no person left; no one left to be the one who had brought about their nirvana? And wouldn't it be incorrect to talk about the parts to a person having been destroyed, at a point in time when the person was still present? How could we have a knowledge of the end?\", \"course\": \"SVN\", \"source_wave\": \"gmr-volumes-2026-08\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 21906,
        "course": "Sunlight",
        "seq": 160,
        "acip": "DE BZHIN GSHEGS PA RNAMS NI THA SNYAD DU SANGS RGYAS PAR 'DOD DGOS MOD, DON DAM PAR SANGS MA RGYAS PA NI, GONG DU BRJOD PA LTAR DE BZHIN GSHEGS PA YANG RANG GI PHUNG PO LA BRTEN NAS BTAGS PA TZAM MA GTOGS, RANG GI ZAG MED KYI PHUNG PO RE RE BA DANG TSOGS PA DANG DE LAS NGO BO THA DAD PA GANG YANG DE BZHIN GSHEGS PA MIN TE,",
        "wylie": "de bzhin gshegs pa rnams ni tha snyad du sangs rgyas par 'dod dgos mod, don dam par sangs ma rgyas pa ni, gong du brjod pa ltar de bzhin gshegs pa yang rang gi phung po la brten nas btags pa tsam ma gtogs, rang gi zag med kyi phung po re re ba dang tshogs pa dang de las ngo bo tha dad pa gang yang de bzhin gshegs pa min te",
        "english": "We would admittedly have to agree that Those Gone Thus have, in a nominal sense, attained enlightenment. But here is how it is that they cannot attain enlightenment in any ultimate sense. As described above, even Those Gone Thus are only persons who are projected upon the various parts to them. There is no One Gone Thus who exists in any other way: who is any one of the immaculate parts to them, individually; or who is the combination of these parts; or who is such that they are independent of these parts either.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"Sunlight\", \"acip\": \"DE BZHIN GSHEGS PA RNAMS NI THA SNYAD DU SANGS RGYAS PAR 'DOD DGOS MOD, DON DAM PAR SANGS MA RGYAS PA NI, GONG DU BRJOD PA LTAR DE BZHIN GSHEGS PA YANG RANG GI PHUNG PO LA BRTEN NAS BTAGS PA TZAM MA GTOGS, RANG GI ZAG MED KYI PHUNG PO RE RE BA DANG TSOGS PA DANG DE LAS NGO BO THA DAD PA GANG YANG DE BZHIN GSHEGS PA MIN TE,\", \"wylie\": \"de bzhin gshegs pa rnams ni tha snyad du sangs rgyas par 'dod dgos mod, don dam par sangs ma rgyas pa ni, gong du brjod pa ltar de bzhin gshegs pa yang rang gi phung po la brten nas btags pa tsam ma gtogs, rang gi zag med kyi phung po re re ba dang tshogs pa dang de las ngo bo tha dad pa gang yang de bzhin gshegs pa min te\", \"english\": \"We would admittedly have to agree that Those Gone Thus have, in a nominal sense, attained enlightenment. But here is how it is that they cannot attain enlightenment in any ultimate sense. As described above, even Those Gone Thus are only persons who are projected upon the various parts to them. There is no One Gone Thus who exists in any other way: who is any one of the immaculate parts to them, individually; or who is the combination of these parts; or who is such that they are independent of these parts either.\"}"
      },
      {
        "id": 22232,
        "course": "Sunlight",
        "seq": 486,
        "acip": "BCOM LDAN 'DAS KYIS BKA' STZAL PA, RAB 'BYOR, DE BAS NA, DE BZHIN GSHEGS PAS CHOS THAMS CAD NI, SEMS CAN MED PA, SROG MED PA, GANG ZAG MED PA'O, ,ZHES GSUNG NGO,,",
        "wylie": "bcom ldan 'das kyis bka' stsal pa, rab 'byor, de bas na, de bzhin gshegs pas chos thams cad ni, sems can med pa, srog med pa, gang zag med pa'o, ,zhes gsung ngo",
        "english": "The Conqueror then said, This is why, o Subhuti, that the One Thus Gone says that all existing things are such that no living being exists, and nothing that lives exists, and no person exists.",
        "raw": "{\"course\": \"Sunlight\", \"acip\": \"BCOM LDAN 'DAS KYIS BKA' STZAL PA, RAB 'BYOR, DE BAS NA, DE BZHIN GSHEGS PAS CHOS THAMS CAD NI, SEMS CAN MED PA, SROG MED PA, GANG ZAG MED PA'O, ,ZHES GSUNG NGO,,\", \"wylie\": \"bcom ldan 'das kyis bka' stsal pa, rab 'byor, de bas na, de bzhin gshegs pas chos thams cad ni, sems can med pa, srog med pa, gang zag med pa'o, ,zhes gsung ngo\", \"english\": \"The Conqueror then said, This is why, o Subhuti, that the One Thus Gone says that all existing things are such that no living being exists, and nothing that lives exists, and no person exists.\"}"
      },
      {
